This is the help documentation for the govax VAX emulator.  Topic sections are
marked by a "$" delimiter.  This is followed by as many tokens of the help
command as were parsed, separated by commas.  Note that if the token is 
less than four characters long, it must be blank padded.  For example, 
"HELP SET TRACE" would resolve to locating the marker "$SET ,TRAC".  The 
"$" must be the first character of the line, and the line can only contain 
the keyword match string.  The text is displayed until the next "$" marker 
is read, or the end of the file is found.

The only exception is if you have more than one possible key tag in a row 
for the same block of text.  For example, "HELP RUN" and "HELP GO" might 
both be tags for the same command if "GO" is a synonym for "RUN". 

$SHOW,LOGI
$SH  ,LOGI
$SHOW,LOG 
$SH  ,LOG 
    The SHOW LOGICAL command displays logical names and their equivalence
    strings, in the same format as VMS.

    Syntax:

         SHOW LOGICAL [logical-name[,...]]

    Qualifiers:

        /PROCESS, /GROUP, /SYSTEM, /TABLE=(table[,...])

            Search only the named table(s).  By default the tables that
            LNM$DCL_LOGICAL names are searched: the process, group, and
            system tables.

        /FULL

            Also show each name's access mode, such as [super] or [exec],
            and each equivalence string's translation attributes
            ([concealed], [terminal]).

        /STRUCTURE

            Show the logical name directories and the tables in each.

    With no logical name, every name in each table searched is listed
    under the table's name.  A logical name containing the * or %
    wildcards lists the matching names the same way.  Any other logical
    name is shown from the first table that has it, followed by the
    translation of each equivalence string that is itself a logical name,
    numbered by level:

         $ SHOW LOGICAL MYDISK
           "MYDISK" = "WORK4" (LNM$PROCESS_TABLE)
         1 "WORK4" = "$255$DUA17:" (LNM$SYSTEM_TABLE)

$SHOW,MEMO
$SHOW,MEM
$SH  ,MEMO
$SH  ,MEM
    The SHOW MEMORY command displays the amount of physical and virtual
    memory available on the virtual VAX.

$SHOW,TRAN
$SH  ,TRAN
    The SHOW TRANSLATION command displays one level of translation of a
    logical name: the first match found, with no further translation.

    Syntax:

         SHOW TRANSLATION [/TABLE=table] logical-name

    Qualifier:

        /TABLE=table

            The table to search, or a logical name that translates to a
            list of tables.  The default is LNM$DCL_LOGICAL (the process,
            group, and system tables).

$DEFI
$DEF 
    The DEFINE command creates a logical name, or replaces an existing
    one defined in the same table at the same access mode.

    Syntax:

         DEFINE logical-name equivalence-string[,...]

    More than one equivalence string makes the name a search list.  Any
    trailing colon is kept as part of the logical name.  Unquoted text is
    converted to uppercase.

    Qualifiers:

        /PROCESS, /GROUP, /SYSTEM, /TABLE=table

            The table to define the name in.  The default is the process
            table, LNM$PROCESS.  /TABLE accepts a table name or a logical
            name that translates to one, such as LNM$PROCESS.

        /USER_MODE, /SUPERVISOR_MODE, /EXECUTIVE_MODE

            The access mode of the name.  The default is supervisor mode.
            A user-mode name in the process table is deleted when the
            next program started with RUN exits.

        /TRANSLATION_ATTRIBUTES=(CONCEALED,TERMINAL)

            CONCEALED shows the logical name in place of the device it
            stands for.  TERMINAL stops iterative translation at this
            name.  The attributes apply to every equivalence string.

        /LOG (default), /NOLOG

            Report when an existing definition is superseded.

    DEFINE/DEVICE, which defines a pseudodevice, is described separately.

$ANAL

    The ANALYZE/OBJECT command describes each record of an object file
    (or of an object library's modules) as VMS's ANALYZE/OBJECT does,
    in the same words and page layout: headers, global symbol directory
    subrecords with their flags, each TIR command with the linker's
    stack depth, STORE IMMEDIATE data as hex and characters, and the
    end of module record.  Errors in the file are flagged with "***"
    where they're found, and counted in a summary at the end.

    Syntax:

        ANALYZE/OBJECT <file>[,...][/HOST] [qualifiers...]

    Parameters:

        <file>          An object file (default type .OBJ), or an object
                        library (.OLB), whose modules are analyzed one
                        after another.  It can be a host file or a file
                        on a mounted volume, by the rules MACRO uses for
                        its source (see HELP MACRO).  A bare name is
                        found beside the file before it.

    Qualifiers:

        /OUTPUT[=<file>]
                        Writes the report to a file instead of the
                        console: by default the first file's name with
                        the type .ANL, beside it.

        /MHD, /GSD, /TIR, /TBT, /DBG, /LNK, /EOM
                        Shows only these kinds of records (/MHD shows
                        every header record).  Every record is still
                        checked and counted in the summary.

        /INCLUDE[=(<module>,...)]
                        Analyzes these modules of an object library;
                        names may hold the wildcards * and %.  Without
                        names, or without /INCLUDE, every module.

    Examples:

        ANALYZE/OBJECT "hello.obj"
        ANALYZE/OBJECT/OUTPUT DUA0:[000000]HELLO
        ANALYZE/OBJECT/GSD/INCLUDE=(MAIN) DUA0:[000000]MINE.OLB

    The ANALYZE/IMAGE command describes an executable or shareable image
    as VMS's ANALYZE/IMAGE does, in the same words and page layout: the
    image header (transfer addresses, symbol table locations, the
    image's name, ident, and link time, and each image section
    descriptor with its flags), then the image activator's fixup
    section (the shareable images the image uses, its G^ and .ADDRESS
    references to them, and the protection changes).  Errors in the
    file are flagged with "***" where they're found and counted at the
    end.

    Syntax:

        ANALYZE/IMAGE <file>[,...][/HOST] [qualifiers...]

    Parameters:

        <file>          An image file (default type .EXE), on the host
                        or on a mounted volume, found as for
                        ANALYZE/OBJECT.

    Qualifiers:

        /OUTPUT[=<file>]
                        Writes the report to a file instead of the
                        console: by default the first file's name with
                        the type .ANI, beside it.

        /HEADER         Shows only the image header.

        /FIXUP_SECTION  Shows the fixup section with the header (the
                        default when neither qualifier is given).

    Examples:

        ANALYZE/IMAGE "prog.exe"
        ANALYZE/IMAGE/HEADER/OUTPUT DUA0:[000000]PROG

$ASSI
    The ASSIGN command creates a logical name, like DEFINE, but with its
    parameters in the opposite order.  One trailing colon is removed from
    the logical name.

    Syntax:

         ASSIGN equivalence-string[,...] logical-name

    ASSIGN takes the same qualifiers as DEFINE.

$DEAS
    The DEASSIGN command deletes a logical name.  One trailing colon is
    removed from the logical name, so to delete a name that ends in a
    colon, give two.

    Syntax:

         DEASSIGN logical-name
         DEASSIGN/ALL

    Qualifiers:

        /PROCESS, /GROUP, /SYSTEM, /TABLE=table

            The table to delete from.  The default is the process table.
            Deleting a table's name from LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY or
            LNM$SYSTEM_DIRECTORY deletes the table and all its descendants:

                 DEASSIGN/TABLE=LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY TAX

        /USER_MODE, /SUPERVISOR_MODE, /EXECUTIVE_MODE

            Delete names at this access mode and any less privileged
            mode.  The default is supervisor mode, so the executive-mode
            names SYS$INPUT, SYS$OUTPUT, SYS$ERROR and SYS$COMMAND are not
            deleted.

        /ALL

            Delete every such name in the table.

$CREA,/NAM
    The CREATE/NAME_TABLE command creates a logical name table.

    Syntax:

         CREATE/NAME_TABLE table-name

    Qualifiers:

        /PARENT_TABLE=table

            The new table's parent.  The default, LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY,
            makes a table private to the process.  A shareable parent
            such as LNM$SYSTEM_DIRECTORY makes a shareable table.

        /USER_MODE, /SUPERVISOR_MODE, /EXECUTIVE_MODE

            The access mode of the table.  The default is supervisor mode.

        /LOG (default), /NOLOG

            Report when an existing table is superseded.

    To have file lookups search the new table, add it to LNM$PROCESS:

         $ DEFINE/TABLE=LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY LNM$PROCESS -
                LNM$PROCESS_TABLE, TAX

$CREA,/DIR
    The CREATE/DIRECTORY command creates a directory on a mounted ODS-2
    volume, as VMS DCL's CREATE/DIRECTORY does, and every directory above
    it that doesn't exist yet: CREATE/DIRECTORY [A.B.C] makes [A], [A.B],
    and [A.B.C], whichever are missing.  A directory that already exists
    is reported, and isn't an error.

    Syntax:

        CREATE/DIRECTORY [qualifiers] <directory>[,<directory>...]

    Parameter:

        <directory>     A directory specification, such as [A.B] or
                        DUA0:[A.B].  It may be relative to the current
                        default directory ([.SUB], [-.OTHER]; see SET
                        DEFAULT), and its device may be a logical name.
                        It can't name a file, or use wildcards.  With no
                        device and no default device on a mounted volume,
                        the directory is made on the host instead,
                        relative to the host's current directory.

    Qualifiers:

        /OWNER_UIC=<uic>
        /OWNER_UIC=PARENT
                        The new directory's owner: a UIC, [group,member]
                        in octal, or PARENT for the owner of the
                        directory it's made in, which is also the
                        default.

        /VERSION_LIMIT=<n>
                        The default version limit for files created in
                        the new directory; 0 means no limit.  The default
                        is the limit of the directory it's made in.

        /PROTECTION=(<category>:<access>,...)
                        The new directory's protection: for each of
                        SYSTEM, OWNER, GROUP, and WORLD (or S, O, G, W),
                        the access granted, any of R, W, E, and D, or
                        none.  The default is the protection of the
                        directory it's made in, less delete access; a
                        category not given keeps that default.

        /ALLOCATION=<n> The number of blocks to give the directory at
                        once.  The default is 1.

        /LOG            Reports the directory made.  The directories
                        made above it aren't reported.

    A /VERSION_LIMIT out of range is reported and ignored, and the
    directory still made, as VMS does.

    Examples:

        CREATE/DIRECTORY [PROJECTS.TEST]

        CREATE/DIRECTORY/LOG/OWNER_UIC=[200,201]/VERSION_LIMIT=3 [USER]

        CREATE/DIRECTORY/PROTECTION=(S:RWE,O:RWE,G:RE,W) [.SHARED]

    See also the help for DIRECTORY and SET DEFAULT.

$CREA
    The CREATE command creates a directory on a mounted volume
    (CREATE/DIRECTORY) or a logical name table (CREATE/NAME_TABLE).
    Creating a file from terminal input, CREATE's form with neither
    qualifier on VMS, isn't supported.  For details, see:

        HELP CREATE /DIRECTORY
        HELP CREATE /NAME_TABLE

$SHOW,DEVI
$SH  ,DEVI
$SHOW,DEV 
$SH  ,DEV 
    The SHOW DEVICE command displays information about a pseudodevice
    attached to the VMS system.  These devices are emulated via VMS
    system service calls that are managed by the emulator when a full
    version of VMS is not running.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW DEVICE [/FULL] [name]
    
    Parameter:
    
        name
        
            The name of a device to display.  If omitted then all
            known psueodevices are displayed.
        
        /FULL
        
            If this qualifier is present then all information about
            the device (essentially the device control block contents)
            is displayed.
 
$SHOW,INST
$SH  ,INST
    The SHOW INSTRUCTION command prints a reminder of the instruction
    opcode values.  It only displays the instructions that are implemented
    in this version of the emulator.  The SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/ALL command
    displays additional information about each instructions operands, if
    any.

    Syntax:

        SHOW INSTRUCTIONS

        SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/ALL

$SHOW,INST,/UNI
$SHOW,UNIM,INST
$SH  ,INST,/UNI
$SH  ,UNIM,INST
    The SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/UNIMPLEMENTED command displays a table of the
    VAX instructions that are *not* currently implemented in the current
    version of govax.  Use of these instructions results in a reserved
    opcode fault.

    Syntax: 

        SHOW INSTRUCTIONS/UNIMPLEMENTED

$SHOW,INST,/PRO
$SH  ,INST,/PRO
    The SHOW INSTRUCTION/PROFILE command displays the number of times
    each VAX instruction has been executed.  This can be used to determine
    where a program is spending it's time, based on the cost of each type
    of instruction.

    You can reset the counters to zero with the CLEAR PROFILE command.

    Syntax:

         SHOW INSTRUCTION/PROFILE

$SHOW,INST,/MOD
$SH  ,INST,/MOD
    The SHOW INSTRUCTION/MODE command displays the number of times each VAX
    instruction mode has been executed.  This can be used to determine
    what addressing modes are used, etc..

    You can reset the counters to zero with the CLEAR PROFILE command.

    Syntax:

         SHOW INSTRUCTION/MODE

$CLEA,MEMO
$CLEA,MEM
$ZERO
    The CLEAR MEMORY command (or ZERO command, which is a synonym) is used
    to zero out the virtual VAX's memory.  All physical memory is cleared,
    which implicitly zeros out virtual memory.  When a CLEAR MEMORY command
    is given, it also turns virtual memory off, since the page tables will 
    have been zeroed.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR MEMORY

$CLEA,SYM ,/ALL
$CLEA,SYMB,/ALL
    The CLEAR SYMBOLS/ALL command deletes all symbols defined so far by the
    user or assembler, and releases the storage occupied by them.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR SYMBOL/ALL

$CLEA,SYM 
$CLEA,SYMB
    The CLEAR SYMBOL command deletes a single symbol definition and releases
    the storage occupied by the symbol.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR SYMBOL <symbol-name>

    Parameters:

        <symbol-name>   The symbol to be deleted.  It can be enclosed in
                        quotes but the quotes are optional.

    Examples:

        CLEAR SYMBOL MAIN_ENTRY

$CLEA
    The CLEAR command is used to clear various attributes of the virtual VAX
    or the console processor.  See the help for

        CLEAR MEMORY
        CLEAR STRINGS
        CLEAR SYMBOL

    Breakpoints, interrupts, and the translation buffer are the debugger's:
    see CANCEL in the debugger's help (DEBUG, then HELP CANCEL).

$INIT

    The INITIALIZE command performs one of two unrelated operations,
    selected by a required qualifier -- INITIALIZE (or its unambiguous
    4-letter abbreviation, INIT) with no qualifier at all is an error.

        INITIALIZE/VAX          (Re-)configures the amount of physical
                                 RAM the virtual VAX is running with.

        INITIALIZE/CONTAINER    Formats a new, empty ODS-2 disk-image
                                 container file for use with MOUNT.

    See the help for INITIALIZE/VAX and INITIALIZE/CONTAINER for the
    syntax of each.

$INIT,/VAX

    The INITIALIZE/VAX command (re-)initializes the virtual VAX, and
    specifies the amount of physical RAM the machine is configured with.
    This storage is allocated by the govax process from its own address
    space.

    Syntax:

        INITIALIZE/VAX <pages>

    Parameter:

        <pages>         The number of physical pages of VAX memory that the
                        virtual machine is configured with.  Each page is
                        512 bytes, so a page count of 1024 would require a
                        half-megabyte virtual machine, for example.

    Example:

        INITIALIZE/VAX 8192   ; Create a 4mb virtual VAX

    See also the help for INITIALIZE and INITIALIZE/CONTAINER.

$INIT,/CON

    The INITIALIZE/CONTAINER command formats a new, empty ODS-2 disk-image
    container file -- a real Files-11 volume, compatible with real VAX/VMS
    and other emulators such as simh.  The new container is not mounted;
    use MOUNT afterward to attach it to a device before using it.

    Syntax:

        INITIALIZE/CONTAINER <file> [label] [/SIZE=<n>] [/DEVICE=<dev>] [/CLUSTER=<n>]

    Parameters:

        <file>          The path to the new disk-image container file on
                        the native file system.  If the native file system
                        is case sensitive, or the name contains embedded
                        blanks, it should be enclosed in quotes to preserve
                        case and spacing.

        <size>          The size of the new volume, in 512-byte blocks.

        [label]         The volume label to record in the container's home
                        block.  If omitted, the volume is labeled NONAME.

    Qualifier:

        /CLUSTER=<n>    The number of blocks per storage cluster.  If
                        omitted, the volume uses 1-block clusters.

        /DEVICE=<dev>   Specify the VAX device type for this container,
                        which sets the maximum block count.
        
        /SIZE=<n>       Specify the maximum block count for the container.
                        This is required if /DEVICE is not specified.

    Example:

        INITIALIZE/CONTAINER "newdisk.dsk" /DEVICE=RD54 WORK01

    See also the help for INITIALIZE, INITIALIZE/VAX, and MOUNT.

$EXIT
$QUIT

    The QUIT command terminates the console processor.  As long as there are
    no pending errors or signals to be processed, the emulator application
    quits.  If there are pending errors, etc. to be displayed, these are
    displayed on the console and the user must manually select the "Quit"
    menu item from the "File" menu.

$LOAD

    The LOAD command reads the contents of a saved memory image file
    into the current virtual VAX.  The LOAD command restores the contents
    of memory and the registers.  It does not restore saved symbols,
    breakpoints, or other console-specific context.   Also see the help
    for the LOAD/ROM command is used to load a ROM image into the console 
    ROM memory, and LOAD/NVRAM to load non-volatile RAM images.

    Syntax:

        LOAD    <file-name>

        
        
    Parameter:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "Saved.VAX"
                        is assumed.
        
        
$LOAD,/ROM

    The LOAD/ROM command reads a ROM file into the special memory area
    in the virtual VAX physical address space for a console ROM.  This
    ROM file can be a text file containing the console EXAM command
    output of the console memory, or a binary image created with the
    SAVE/ROM command.

    Syntax:


        LOAD/ROM [/BASE=<base-addr>] [/SIZE=<size-in-k>] <filename>
        
            or
        
        LOAD/ROM/NOERROR
        
        
    Parameters:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "default.rom"
                        is assumed.
        
        <base-addr>     When loading a ROM image, this specifies
                        the base physical address of the ROM image
                        to use.  If ommitted, 20040000 is assumed.
        
        <size-in-k>     This is the size of the ROM memory in kilobytes.
                        If ommitted, the default is ^d256 for a 256K ROM.

        Note that /BASE and /SIZE cannot be used if the ROM file being
        read is a ROM image created by SAVE/ROM; this has a fixed address
        and size associated with it.
        
        The second example (using the /NOERROR qualifier) indicates that if
        the default ROM file cannot be found then no error is displayed.  This
        allows the LOAD/ROM/NOERROR command to be placed in the initialization
        file, and not interfere with startup if a ROM file is not found.
$LOAD,/NVR

    The LOAD/NVRAM command reads a non-volatile RAM file into the special 
    memory area in the virtual VAX physical address space for the NVRAM.
    The NVRAM area exists on some VAX systems to contain console boot data,
    etc. and is used by the console ROM on those systems.  The NVRAM file
    is created with the SAVE/NVRAM command after INIT/NVRAM has first
    allocated it.
    

    Syntax:


        LOAD/NVRAM  <filename>
        
            or
        
        LOAD/NVRAM/NOERROR
        
        
    Parameters:

        <file-name>     The name of the file to be read.  If
                        omitted, the default name of "default.nvram"
                        is assumed.
        
        
        The second example (using the /NOERROR qualifier) indicates that if
        the default NVRAM file cannot be found then no error is displayed.  This
        allows the LOAD/NVRAM/NOERROR command to be placed in the initialization
        file, and not interfere with startup if a NVRAM file is not found.
$INIT,/NVR

    The INIT/NVRAM command creates an empty non-volatile RAM area for use with
    a console ROM file.  The console ROM should contain code to initialize the
    NVRAM to a consistent state.  Use the SAVE/NVRAM command to save the resulting
    NVRAM file.  
    

    Syntax:


        INIT/NVRAM [/BASE=<base-addr>] [/SIZE=<size-in-k>]
                
        
    Parameters:

        
        <base-addr>     When creating an NVRAM area, this specifies
                        the base physical address of the NVRAM
                        to use.  If ommitted, 20080000 is assumed.
        
        <size-in-k>     This is the size of the NVRAM memory in kilobytes.
                        If ommitted, the default is 1 for a 1K NVRAM.

$SAVE

    The SAVE command writes the current memory contents to a binary file.
    This file can later be reloaded with the LOAD command.  For help on
    saving the entire machine state to a text file, see the help on
    SAVE/TEXT.  You can also use the SAVE/ROM command to save a ROM image.

    Note that the memory image for the SAVE command is not just a file
    image of the memory.  It contains a header of approxamately 1K of
    data describing the state of the virtual VAX at the time the SAVE
    command is issued.  This is followed by zero or more page ranges,
    where the address and number of pages is stored followed by that
    many pages of data.  The SAVE command only saves non-zero pages.
    
    Syntax:

        SAVE  <file-path>

    Parameter:

        <file-name>     This is the name of the file that the memory
                        image is saved to.  If omitted, the default
                        name of "Saved.VAX" is assumed.

    See also:

        SAVE/ROM        Save the current ROM storage as a file
        SAVE/NVRAM      Save the current NVRAM storage as a file

$SAVE,/ROM

    The SAVE/ROM command writes system ROM to an external file.

    Syntax:

        SAVE/ROM <file-path>

    Parameter:

        <file-path>     This is the name of the file where the emulator
                        ROM image is to be saved.  The name can be enclosed in
                        quotation marks if it contains imbedded blanks.
                        The file will be overwritten if it already exists.
    
    Example:

        SAVE/ROM "current.rom"

$SAVE,/NVR

    The SAVE/NVRAM command writes system NVRAM storage to an external file.

    Syntax:

        SAVE/NVRAM <file-path>

    Parameter:

        <file-path>     This is the name of the file where the emulator
                        NVRAM image is to be saved.  The name can be enclosed in
                        quotation marks if it contains imbedded blanks.
                        The file will be overwritten if it already exists.
    
    Example:

        SAVE/NVRAM "latest.nvram"


$@   
$INCL

    The INCLUDE command (which can be abbreviated to "@") is used to read 
    an external text file as a set of commands, as if they were typed by 
    the user at the console.  It can be used to store a set of often-
    repeated commands, for example.  It can also be used to retrieve a 
    stored VAX state created by the SAVE TEXT command, since the saved 
    state can be expressed as a series of commands that will re-create 
    the virtual VAX.
    
    Syntax:
    
        INCLUDE <file-path>
    
    
    Parameter:
    
        <file-path>     This is the name of the text file to read.  It must
                        already exist or an error occurs.
    
    Example:
    
        INCLUDE "myvax.status"

    As in every DCL command, a host file name with lowercase letters or
    a "/" must be in quotes; DCL uppercases unquoted text.


$EXEC
$GO  

    The GO command is used to invoke the emulator and begin execution of a stream 
    of instructions in the virtual VAX.  The command begins execution at the current
    value of the PC register, and can optionally specify a starting address.

    The program runs until it halts.  If a breakpoint or a fault breakpoint
    stops it first, the debugger starts and its prompt, DBG>, appears; the
    program is stopped, and the debugger's commands (EXAMINE, STEP, GO, ...)
    work on it.  EXIT at DBG> returns to the console.  Breakpoints are set
    from the debugger (see DEBUG), before or after the program starts.
    Ctrl/C stops the program and returns to the console's prompt; DEBUG
    then examines it (see HELP KEYS).
    
    Syntax:
    
        GO [<address>]
    
    Parameters:
    
        <address>       The starting address.  This can be a numeric value in
                        the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol enclosed in
                        single quotes.
                        
    
    Examples:
    
        GO
        
        GO 00000200
        
        GO ENTRY_PT

$ASM ,PSEU,SHIM
$ASSE,PSEU,SHIM
    The .SHIM pseudo-operand creates a software shim entry.  This is used in
    the micro-kernel to map runtime library vector points to support routines
    in the micro-kernel.  Some of these support routines are implemented in
    the micro-kernel itself, and some transfer control to native runtime code
    in the govax emulator.
    
    Syntax:
    
        .SHIM   <entry-name>, [<runtime-code>], <runtime-library>, <offset>
    
    Parameters:
    
        <entry-name>        This is the name of the entry point that supports
                            the runtime function.  It may be the name of an
                            already-defined entry point in the micro-kernel,
                            or a shim stub will be generated by default with
                            this name.
        
        <runtime-code>      If specified, this is the dispatch code for the
                            integrated runtime library in the native emulator
                            code.  See the files shim.c and rtl.c for more
                            information.  If omitted or zero, then a shim
                            stub is not create, and entry-name is assumed to
                            specify a real VAX support routine in the micro-
                            kernel.
        
        <runtime-library>   The name of the runtime library that this is an
                            entry point shim for, such as LIBRTL or DECC$SHR.
                            Do not include the ".EXE" suffix.  This name must
                            match the name of a sharable library list entry
                            in an image for the shim to be bound to the image.
        
        <offset>            This is the offset into the sharable image library
                            where the routine resides.  Usually this is an
                            offset near the start of the actual sharable image
                            code that is a jump vector to the "real" routine.
                            In any case, this offset value (a longword) must
                            exactly match the offset in the image to be bound.
    
    Example:
    
        .SHIM   LIB$PUT_OUTPUT, 0, LIBRTL, ^X0478
        
        This indicates that when an image is loaded that includes a sharable
        image reference to LIBRTL+^X0478, then those references will be remapped
        to the LIB$PUT_OUTPUT routine.  Because the runtime library dispatch 
        code is zero, the routine must be a VAX routine, already assembled in 
        memory.
        
        
        .SHIM   STR$UPCASE, 2, LIBRTL, ^X0778
        
        This indicates that when an image is loaded with a reference to 
        LIBRTL+^X0778, it will call a shim stub named STR$UPCASE.  The shim stub 
        is created as part of the assembly of the .SHIM instruction.  This stub
        will use the special XFC instruction to transfer control to the RTL 
        support routine for code 2.  See shim.c and rtl.c for more information.
        
$RUN 
$RUN ,/DEB
    The RUN command loads a VAX/VMS image into memory, and executes it.  This
    command assumes that a VMINIT has been run, and the standard micro-kernel
    (kernel.asm) has been loaded and initialized.  
    
    The command will load a valid, normal VAX/VMS image into memory.  References
    to sharable images must be resolved using "software shims".  These are stubs
    that map sharable image vector slots to routines in memory, typically supplied
    by the micro-kernel.  Some shims may redirect control to native runtime code
    via a special XFC instruction.  See the help on ASM PSEUDO SHIM or SHOW SHIM
    for more information.
    
    If the image has a transfer address, then the image is started at it's user
    transfer address.  If there is no transfer address, the image is loaded but
    not executed.  
    
    If the optional /DEBUG qualifier is used, the image is loaded and then run
    under the debugger, which stops it at the first instruction of its main
    routine (after the entry mask) and prompts with DBG>.  GO continues it;
    STEP, SET BREAK, EXAMINE, and the rest are the debugger's commands.  An
    image linked with /DEBUG (LINK/DEBUG) starts the debugger the same way,
    unless RUN/NODEBUG says not to.  An image linked /NOTRACEBACK has no symbols for
    the debugger, and runs without it.  /STEP and /BREAK are
    synonyms of /DEBUG, and /NOEXECUTE loads the image without running it.
    
    Syntax:
    
        RUN [/DEBUG|/NODEBUG] [/NOEXECUTE] <image-name>[/HOST]
    
    Parameter:
    
        <image-name>    An image file, on the native system or on a mounted
                        ODS-2 volume, by the same rules as MACRO's and LINK's
                        files (see HELP MACRO): a VMS file specification
                        (DUA0:[PROGS]HELLO.EXE), or a bare name while SET
                        DEFAULT is on a volume, is a file on the volume, with
                        the type EXE if it has none.  Anything else, or any
                        name followed by /HOST, is a native file.  An image
                        made on a VAX can be copied to the native system in
                        binary mode, or read in place from its disk.
                        
                        If the native file system is case sensitive, the name may 
                        need to be enclosed in quotes to preserve case.
                        
                        The image is mapped into memory and executed.  The
                        shareable images it uses are always looked for on
                        the native system; govax's shims stand in for any
                        that aren't there.

    RUN gives the image no command text: LIB$GET_FOREIGN returns an empty
    string.  To pass an image text, run it as a foreign command (see HELP
    FOREIGN), or give the text after the file name on govax's own command
    line ("govax run forth.exe 2 3 + .").

$SYMB
    A symbol assignment gives a name to a string, as DCL's do.  When the first
    word of a command is a symbol, its value replaces the word before the
    command is read.  A value beginning with "$" makes the symbol a foreign
    command (see HELP FOREIGN); any other value is the start of a command, so
    a symbol can abbreviate or rename one.

    Syntax:

        symbol-name :=  string            symbol-name =  expression
        symbol-name :== string            symbol-name == expression

    The string is the rest of the line: outside quotes, letters are made
    uppercase, runs of blanks become one, and a "!" begins a comment; quotes
    keep text as written, and are removed ("" inside them is a quote).  The
    expression is a quoted string or a decimal integer.

    An "*" in the name says how short an abbreviation still means the symbol:
    after "FO*RTH :== $FORTH", FO, FOR, FORT, and FORTH all do.

    The console keeps one table of symbols, so ":=" and ":==" are the same,
    as are "=" and "==", but SHOW SYMBOL/DCL shows which each symbol was
    assigned with.  These are DCL symbols, not the VAX symbols ASM, CALL,
    and SHOW SYMBOL use.  SHOW SYMBOL/DCL displays them, and DELETE/SYMBOL
    deletes one.

    Examples:

        SD :== SET DEFAULT
        FORTH :== $DUA0:[TOOLS]FORTH.EXE
        GREETING == "Hello, ""World"""

$FORE
    A foreign command runs an image with the rest of the command line as its
    command text, which the image reads with LIB$GET_FOREIGN.  Define one with
    a symbol whose value is "$" and the image's file name (see HELP SYMBOL):

        FORTH :== $DUA0:[TOOLS]FORTH.EXE
        FORTH 2 3 + . CR

    The image is found as RUN finds it.  The command text is treated as DCL
    treats a command's parameters: outside quotes it is made uppercase and its
    blanks are compressed, and a "!" begins a comment; quoted text, quotes and
    all, is passed as typed.

$DELE,/SYM
$DEL ,/SYM
    The DELETE/SYMBOL command deletes a symbol (see HELP SYMBOL).

    Syntax:

        DELETE/SYMBOL [/GLOBAL | /LOCAL] symbol-name

    The name may be any abbreviation the symbol allows.  /GLOBAL and /LOCAL
    are accepted; the console has one table of symbols.

$CALL
    The CALL command is used to invoke the emulator and begin execution of a
    stream of instructions in the virtual VAX.  The command begins execution
    at the address supplied in the command by invoking it with a CALLS
    procedure call.  The command can pass a varying number of longword 
    parameters to the procedure if desired.

    The return value of the procedure is available in R0, and is also copied
    to the variable $STATUS.

    As with GO, a breakpoint that stops the procedure starts the debugger
    at DBG>, and Ctrl/C returns to the console's prompt.
    
    Syntax:
    
        CALL <address> [ ( arg1 [, arg2...] ) ]
    
    Parameters:
    
        <address>       The starting address.  This can be a numeric value in
                        the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol.  Because
                        the code is invoked with a CALLS instruction, this
                        must be the address of an entry mask word.  The code
                        stream should end with a RET instruction.
        
        <argn>          Up to fifteen arguments can be supplied to the
                        routine being called.  The CALL command simulates a
                        CALLS instruction, so a stack frame is created with
                        the argument list on the stack.  The arguments are
                        pushed as longwords on the stack and must all be
                        integers.  If arguments are passed they must be
                        enclosed in parenthesis.  If more than one argument
                        is present, separate them with commas.
                        
    
    Examples:
    
        CALL 0200
        
        CALL SUM( ^D35, ^D88 )

$CALL,/STE
    The CALL/STEP command is used to invoke the emulator and begin execution of a
    stream of instructions in the virtual VAX.  This is similar to the function of
    the CALL command, except that the /STEP qualifier indicates that the
    debugger should only step to the first instruction, and then prompts
    (DBG>).  The command begins execution
    at the address supplied in the command by invoking it with a CALLS
    procedure call.  The command can pass a varying number of longword 
    parameters to the procedure if desired.  However, only the first instruction
    of the function is executed.  You must use a GO or STEP command to continue
    execution (STEP or GO at the DBG> prompt).

    
    Syntax:
    
        CALL/STEP <address> [ ( arg1 [, arg2...] ) ]
    
    Parameters:
    
        <address>       The starting address.  This can be a numeric value in
                        the current RADIX, or it can be a symbol.  Because
                        the code is invoked with a CALLS instruction, this
                        must be the address of an entry mask word.  The code
                        stream should end with a RET instruction.
        
        <argn>          Up to fifteen arguments can be supplied to the
                        routine being called.  The CALL command simulates a
                        CALLS instruction, so a stack frame is created with
                        the argument list on the stack.  The arguments are
                        pushed as longwords on the stack and must all be
                        integers.  If arguments are passed they must be
                        enclosed in parenthesis.  If more than one argument
                        is present, separate them with commas.
                        
    
    Examples:
    
        CALL 0200
        
        CALL SUM( ^D35, ^D88 )


$SET 

    The SET command is used to set the state of information in the virtual
    VAX or the console processor.  See the help text on:

        SET ASSEMBLER
        SET DEBUG
        SET DEFAULT
        SET PROMPT
        SET QUANTUM
        SET RADIX
        SET SYMBOL
        SET VERIFY

    The settings of the machine itself (breakpoints, stepping, the PSL, page
    table entries, the base address, the display modes) are the debugger's:
    see HELP SET in the debugger (type DEBUG, then HELP SET).

$SET ,SYMB
$SET ,SYM 

    The SET command can be used to set a symbol value.  Optionally, 
    you can specify attributes of the symbol to be set.  If the symbol
    name already exists, the command updates the value.  If the symbol
    does not already exist, it is created.

    Syntax:

        SET [qualifiers] <symbol> = <value>

    Parameters:

        <symbol>        The name of the symbol to set.  If the name
                        contains a "$" character then it is a system
                        symbol and goes in the system table.  Otherwise
                        it is a user symbol.

        <value>         Any expression.  The expression is evaluated and
                        the result stored in the symbol.

    Qualifiers:

        /PERMANENT      The symbol is marked permanent.  If it is in
                        the system symbol table it will not be deleted,
                        even if all other symbols are removed.

        /LABEL          The symbol is a label of a memory location, and
                        can be used during disassembly to replace a numberic
                        address value.

        /ENTRY          The symbol is an entry point of a two-byte entry
                        mask.

$SET ,DEBU

    The SET DEBUG command sets various debugging switches in the console
    or emulator.  To set the debug switch, use it's name shown below.  To
    turn off the switch, previx the name with NO.

    Syntax:

        SET DEBUG [NO]<name>

    Parameter:

        <name>   The name of a debugging switch.  The values currently
                 supported are:

                 DEBUG         Switch to native debugger if available.

                 VM            Enable debugging messages for virtual memory
                               translations.

                 TB            Enable debugging messages for translation
                               buffer caching.

                 SYMBOLS       Enable debugging messages for symbol
                               creation or resolution.

                 REGISTERS     Enable display of registers that change
                               value on a STEP command.

                 INTERRUPTS    Enable debugging messages for interrupts.

                 EXCEPTIONS    Enable debugging messages for exceptions.

                 IMAGES        Enabled SYS$IMGACT mapping when RUN
                               command issued for an image.

                 USERHALT      Enable HALT instruction in USER mode.

                 KEYBOARD      Debug console keyboard IO activity.

                 SERVICES      Debug P1 vector calls to VMS system services.

                 DCL           Debug DCL command line parsing in console.

                 CHM           Enable debug messages on CHMx or REI.

                 COMMAND       Display command line expansions after symbol
                               substitution in the console.

                 LOGICALS      Debug emulated VMS logical name support.

                 DEVICES       Debug emulated VMS device support.

                 LIBINIT       Enable LIB$INITIALIZE calls on image load.


$SET ,ASM 
$SET ,ASSE

    The SET ASSEMBLER command is used to control characteristics of the
    built-in instruction assembler in the console.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET ASSEMBLER <flag>
    
    Parameters:
    
            
        [NO]ADDRESS_PROMPT
        
            When set, this flag tells the pseudo-assembler to display
            the memory address in which the instruction(s) will be 
            written as part of the prompt for input.

        [NO]FORWARD_WARNINGS
        
            When set, this flag tells the assembler to issue a warning
            whenever the assembler finishes processing statements and
            there are unresolved forward references.  These symbols
            must be resolved before the console will allow a RUN
            statement to execute.

        [NO]BRANCH_DEST

            When set, disassembly of instructions will include a
            comment field that lists the numeric destinations of
            symbolic addresses.  This can make reading the disassembly
            somewhat easier.

        [NO]SYMBOLS

            When set, the disassembler will attempt to convert branch
            and address references back into symbolic names if there
            is a label or entry with a matching value.

        [NO]RESOLVE_TEMP

            When set, the assembler allows symbols prefixed with a "_"
            character to be considered "temporary" with the definition
            of a .ENTRY procedure.  When the end of the entry is
            assembled, the temporary names are qualified by the name of
            the entry that contains them, making them globally unique.

        [NO]SCOPENAMES

            When set, temporary symbols (prefixed with "_") are made
            global by qualifying them with the name of the ENTRY that
            contains them.  When clear, a unique integer number is
            generated instead to define the scope.  This makes the name
            of the symbol shorter (and easier to read in disassembly)
            but less clear which entry it belongs to.


$SET ,SYM
$SET ,SYMB

    The SET command can be used to set a symbol value.  The symbol value is
    then usable anywhere an address can be specified, in a console command
    or an assembler statement.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET <symbol> = <value>
    
    
    Parameters:
    
        <symbol>        The name of the symbol to be set.  Do not enclose the
                        symbol name in single quotes (that is used to mean
                        "resolve the symbol").
        
        <value>         Any numeric valid or a symbol value enclosed in single
                        quotes.  This can also be any integer expression.

    Examples:
    
        SET MAIN = 0200         ; Set MAIN to 0200 (in current RADIX)
        
        SET MAIN = INIT + 2     ; Set MAIN to value of INIT plus two

$SET ,VERI
$SET ,NOVE

    The SET VERIFY command determines whether the text file(s) read with the
    INCLUDE command are displayed on the console as they are processed.  For
    small text files this can be a useful debugging tool.  However, for large
    files (particularly large SAVE files) this may be too slow.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET [NO]VERIFY
    
    Examples:
    
        SET VERIFY
        
        SET NOVERIFY

$SET ,RAD 
$SET ,RADI

    The SET RADIX command is used to set the default radix (numeric base) of
    constant values that do not have an explicit radix indicator like X^ or
    D^.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SET RADIX <base>

    Parameter:
    
        <base>          The specified radix.  Valid values are HEXADECIMAL
                        or DECIMAL.

    Examples:
    
        SET RADIX HEXADECIMAL       ; constant will be interpreted as hex
                                    ; numeric values as if the X^ prefix
                                    ; had been used.

$SHOW,DEBU
$SH  ,DEBU

    The SHOW DEBUG command displays the settings of the internal debug
    switches.  These are set with the SET DEBUG command.  See the help
    for SET DEBUG for more information.

    Syntax:

        SHOW DEBUG

$SHOW,QUAN
$SH  ,QUAN

    The SHOW QUANTUM command displays the execution quantum the emulator
    guarantees before allowing VAX interrupts to be processed. This is
    expressed as a number of instructions; after that many instructions
    have been executed, the processes any queued interrupts.  A quantum
    of zero disables the quantum mechanism, and uses the realtime clock
    instead to signal when to handle interrupts. The use of quantum is
    largely to ensure that interrupt behavior is deterministic.
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW QUANTUM

$SET ,QUAN

    The SET QUANTUM command sets the execution quantum the emulator
    guarantees before allowing VAX interrupts to be processed. This is
    expressed as a number of instructions; after that many instructions
    have been executed, the processes any queued interrupts.  A quantum
    of zero disables the quantum mechanism, and uses the realtime clock
    instead to signal when to handle interrupts. The use of quantum is
    largely to ensure that interrupt behavior is deterministic.
   
    Syntax:
    
        SET QUANTUM n
    
    Parameter:
    
        n       The number of instructions to be executed before the 
                emulator will process interrupts deterministically.

$SHOW,RADI
$SHOW,RAD 
$SH  ,RADI
$SH  ,RAD 

    The SHOW RADIX command displays the default radix being used by the
    console for input.  Valid values are DECIMAL or HEXADECIMAL, and can
    be set by the SET RADIX command.  (The assembler always uses decimal;
    see HELP ASM EXPRESSIONS.)
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW RADIX

$SHOW,SYMB
$SHOW,SYM 
$SH  ,SYMB
$SH  ,SYM 

    The SHOW SYMBOL command can be used to display the value of a specific
    symbol in the symbol table.  You can also use the SHOW SYMBOL/ALL
    command to display all symbols, the SHOW SYMBOL/SYSTEM command to
    display only system symbols, or the SHOW SYMBOL/UNRESOLVED command
    to display symbols that do not yet have an assigned value.

    The predefined system symbols (such as PTE$K_UR, VAX$PR_SBR, and
    OPC$_MOVL) are shown along with the symbols in the symbol table.

    These are the console's VAX symbols.  SHOW SYMBOL/DCL shows the DCL
    symbols assignments define instead (see HELP SHOW SYMBOL /DCL).
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW SYMBOL <name>
    
    Parameter:
    
        name        The name of a symbol to be displayed.  If the symbol
                    does not exist then an error message is displayed.
                    The name can contain the wildcards "*" (any number of
                    characters, including none) and "%" (exactly one
                    character); every symbol whose name matches is then
                    listed, and an error message is displayed if none do.
    
    Examples:
    
        SHOW SYMBOL PTE$K_UR*
        SHOW SYMBOL VAX$PR_%%R

$SHOW,SYMB,/DCL
$SHOW,SYM ,/DCL
$SH  ,SYMB,/DCL
$SH  ,SYM ,/DCL

    The SHOW SYMBOL/DCL command displays DCL symbols: the ones symbol
    assignments define, such as foreign commands (see HELP SYMBOL and
    HELP FOREIGN), as DCL's SHOW SYMBOL displays them.  A global
    symbol's assignment is shown with "==" and a local one's with "=";
    an "*" marks the shortest abbreviation of the name; an integer is
    shown in decimal, hexadecimal, and octal.

    Syntax:

        SHOW SYMBOL/DCL [name]

    Parameter:

        name        The symbol to display, or an abbreviation it allows.
                    With the wildcards "*" and "%", every DCL symbol
                    whose name matches; with no name, every DCL symbol.

    Examples:

        SHOW SYMBOL/DCL
              FO*RTH == "$DUA0:[TOOLS]FORTH.EXE"
              N = 42   Hex = 0000002A  Octal = 00000000052
        SHOW SYMBOL/DCL FO

$SHOW,SYMB,/ALL
$SHOW,SYM ,/ALL
$SH  ,SYMB,/ALL
$SH  ,SYM ,/ALL
$SHOW,SYMB,/SYS
$SHOW,SYM ,/SYS
$SH  ,SYMB,/SYS
$SH  ,SYM ,/SYS

    The SHOW SYMBOL/ALL command will display the settings of all
    symbols.  The SHOW SYMBOL/SYSTEM command displays only the system
    symbols.  Either can be limited to the symbols whose names match a
    name that contains the wildcards "*" (any number of characters,
    including none) and "%" (exactly one character).
    
    Syntax:
    
        SHOW SYMBOLS/ALL [<name>]
        SHOW SYMBOLS/SYSTEM [<name>]
    
    Example:
    
        SHOW SYMBOL/SYSTEM CONSOLE$*

$SHOW
$SH

    The SHOW command is used to display information about the virtual
    VAX or the console processor.  For additional SHOW commands, see
    the help for:
    
        SHOW DEFAULT
        SHOW DEVICE
        SHOW INSTRUCTION
        SHOW INSTRUCTION/PROFILE
        SHOW INSTRUCTION/MODE
        SHOW INSTRUCTION/UNIMPLEMENTED
        SHOW LOGICAL
        SHOW MEMORY
        SHOW PROCESS
        SHOW QUANTUM
        SHOW RADIX
        SHOW STRINGS
        SHOW SYMBOL
        SHOW SYSTEM
        SHOW TRANSLATION

    SHOW also displays the state of the machine itself (registers, memory,
    the PSL, breakpoints, the call stack, page tables, ...), but those are
    the debugger's commands.  Type DEBUG to enter the debugger, then
    HELP SHOW there.

$SHOW,STRI
$SH  ,STRI
    The SHOW STRINGS command displays the string pool managed by the
    assembler.  This allows you to use quoted strings in expressions
    where an address value is normally used.  The assembler creates
    the string in a reserved area of memory (the string pool) and
    substibutes the address of a descriptor for the string in the
    expression.

    Syntax:

        SHOW STRINGS


    Usage:

        To use a string constant, place it in an assembly statement
        anywhere you would use an address expression.  For example,
        the following writes a string to the console using the
        Microkernel.

             PUSHL     I^#0A
             PUSHL     I^#0D
             PUSHL     I^#"This is a test"
             CALLS     #3, @#LIB$PUT_OUTPUT

        The use of the quoted string for the longword value causes a
        descriptor to be created for the string value (stored in a
        reserved area of S0 space defined by the CONSOLE$STRINGPOOL_BASE
        symbol) and the descriptor address is pushed on the stack.

        Note that the value use is _always_ a descriptor address, not
        the address of the string constant itself.

$CLEA,STRI
    The CLEAR STRINGS command resets the string pool.  This invalidates
    any string values used, so the command should only be used when
    clearing programs from memory, etc.   See the SHOW STRINGS command
    for information on the string pool.

    Syntax:

        CLEAR STRINGS

$CONF
$CONFIG

    govax keeps configuration settings in a profile in the .govax
    directory of your home directory.  They are read each time govax
    starts.  The settings are set and shown with the govax command's
    CONFIG verb, from the host shell, not from the VAX> prompt:

        govax config set KEY=VALUE      Set a setting.  A KEY alone,
                                        with no =VALUE, sets it to true.
        govax config show               Show every setting in the profile.
        govax config show KEY           Show one setting's value.
        govax config delete KEY         Remove a setting, so its default
                                        applies again.
        govax config list               List the profiles.

    The global option --profile NAME (before the verb) chooses a profile
    other than the default one, for example:

        govax --profile test config set vax.quantum=50
        govax --profile test

    For example, to have govax create (the first time) and mount a work
    volume each time it starts, and make it the default:

        govax config set vax.default.volume.file="~/vax/work.dsk"

    Keys are case-sensitive and written in lowercase.  govax warns at
    exit about any key in the profile it doesn't recognize, which usually
    means a misspelling.

    For the list of keys and what each one does, type:

        HELP CONFIG KEYS

$CONF,KEYS
$CONF,KEY

    These are the configuration settings govax reads (see HELP CONFIG
    for how to set them).

    The default volume

        When vax.default.volume.file is set, govax mounts that ODS-2
        container at startup and does a SET DEFAULT to it, so file
        commands (DIRECTORY, TYPE, MACRO, LINK, RUN, ...) work on the
        volume rather than on the host's files.  A container that doesn't
        exist is created and initialized first, with the default
        directory in it:

            %MOUNT-I-CREATED, container /home/me/vax/work.dsk created as
                an RD54 volume labeled WORK
            %MOUNT-I-MOUNTED, WORK mounted on _DUA0:

        A container that can only be read is mounted read-only, and
        %MOUNT-I-WRITELOCK says so.  If the volume can't be used (it
        can't be created, opened, or mounted, its label doesn't match,
        or the device is already mounted with --mount), a
        %MOUNT-W-NODEFAULT warning says why and govax starts without it.
        With no container configured, file names go to the host as they
        always have.

        vax.default.volume.file       The container's host path.  A
                                      leading ~/ is your home directory.
                                      Unset: no default volume.

        vax.default.volume.label      The volume label a new container is
                                      given.  When set, an existing
                                      container's label must match it, or
                                      the volume isn't used.  Default WORK
                                      (and no check).

        vax.default.volume.device     The device the volume is mounted on.
                                      Default DUA0:.

        vax.default.volume.type       The disk type (RX50, RD54, RA81, ...)
                                      that sizes a new container, and that
                                      the device is defined as.  Default
                                      RD54 (311,200 blocks).

        vax.default.volume.directory  The default directory, created in
                                      a new container's MFD.  If it isn't
                                      on the volume, the default is the
                                      MFD, [000000].  Default [WORK].

    Files and libraries

        vax.path                      Host directories to search for
                                      files a console command names, after
                                      the name as given, separated by
                                      commas (like the --path option).

        vax.library                   The host directory LINK and MACRO
                                      look in for the system object and
                                      macro libraries and shareable
                                      images, when SYS$LIBRARY and
                                      SYS$SHARE don't lead to them.

        vax.link.library              The older name for vax.library,
                                      read when vax.library isn't set.

    The console

        vax.console.prompt            The console's prompt.  Default
                                      "VAX> ".  SET PROMPT changes it.

        vax.console.lines             The screen's height in lines for
                                      TYPE/PAGE, used when the terminal's
                                      own size can't be read (output not
                                      to a terminal).  Default 24.

        vax.disassemble.symbolic      true or false: whether DISASSEMBLE
                                      and SHOW CALLS show the VMS
                                      debugger's symbolic layout by
                                      default.  Default true.

    The machine

        vax.hardware.clock            true or false: drive the interval
                                      clock and time-of-year clock from
                                      the host's real time rather than
                                      from instruction counts.  Default
                                      false.

        vax.quantum                   The number of instructions between
                                      clock ticks when vax.hardware.clock
                                      is false.  Default 20.

    Processes

        These are for running several processes at once, which govax is
        being extended to do.  govax reads them, but until that work is
        complete they change nothing.

        vax.process.scheduler         true or false: whether processes
                                      other than the console's own can be
                                      created and run.  Default false.

        vax.process.quantum           The number of instructions a
                                      process runs before another process
                                      of the same priority may have the
                                      CPU.  Default 20000.

        vax.process.preempt           Which access modes a process can
                                      lose the CPU in, when its quantum
                                      ends or a higher-priority process
                                      can run: all (as on VMS), user
                                      (user mode only), or none (only
                                      when the process waits).  Default
                                      all; govax warns at startup about
                                      any other value.

    Debugging switches

        vax.debug.NAME                true or false: the initial state of
                                      the SET DEBUG switch NAME (see HELP
                                      SET DEBUG).  NAME is one of vm, tb,
                                      symbols, exceptions, interrupts,
                                      chm, registers, fulldisasm,
                                      userhalt, keyboard, images,
                                      services, dcl, command, logicals,
                                      devices, process, libinit, rms, or
                                      userstep.

$KEYS
$KEY 

    govax gives the control keys the meanings VMS gives them:

    Ctrl/C      Stops the command running, such as a RUN or GO, and
                returns to the prompt of the command line that started it:
                the console's, or the debugger's DBG>.  A program waiting
                for a line of input stops too.  At a prompt, it cancels
                the line being typed.  A program that has enabled a CTRL/C
                AST gets the AST instead (or its CTRL/Y AST, if it has
                only that).  govax echoes *Interrupt*.

    Ctrl/Y      Ends govax, returning to the host's shell: the command
                running stops, mounted volumes are dismounted, and the
                terminal is put back as it was.  If govax doesn't end (a
                command that doesn't finish), a second Ctrl/Y exits at
                once.  A program that has enabled a CTRL/Y AST gets the
                AST instead.  govax echoes *Interrupt*.

    Ctrl/Z      End of file.  On an empty line, the read waiting gets
                the end of file, and govax echoes *Exit*.  Typed after
                some text, it ends the line as RETURN does, and the next
                read is the end of file.  A program's read sees end of
                file (RMS$_EOF from $GET).  At the debugger's DBG> it is
                EXIT, ending the session; in the interactive assembler
                (ASM>) it is .END.  At the console's prompt it only
                echoes *Exit*, as at DCL's: govax ends with EXIT, QUIT,
                or Ctrl/Y.

    The other control keys keep their host meanings: the line editor's
    at a prompt, and the host terminal's while a program runs.  Nothing
    suspends govax.

$HELP

    govax a VAX software emulator.  It supports execution of a subset
    of the VAX instruction set.  It also contains emulation of VAX hardware,
    memory management, and console functions.  The console functions are
    extended beyond existing VAX implementation to include an assembler
    and a debugger, modeled on the VMS debugger, with breakpoints,
    stepping, and commands to examine and change the machine.

    You can get help on a topic by using the HELP command, followed by
    one or more keywords.  For example, to get help on the SHOW command,
    issue the command:

           HELP SHOW

    To get help on the SHOW LOGICAL subcommand to SHOW, use the command:

           HELP SHOW LOGICAL



    For additional information, see the help on the following command topics:

    ANALYZE         Describe object and image files, as VMS's ANALYZE does
    ASM             Assemble VAX source into memory
    ASSIGN          Create a logical name (equivalence first)
    CALL            Execute instructions by calling into a procedure
    CLEAR           Clear memory, symbols, strings
    COPY            Copy a file between the host and a mounted volume
    CREATE          Create a directory or a logical name table
    DEASSIGN        Delete a logical name
    DEFINE          Create a logical name
    DELETE          Delete a file on a mounted volume
    DIRECTORY       List the files on a mounted volume
    DEBUG           Start the debugger on the virtual VAX
    DISMOUNT        Detach a device's mounted disk-image container
    DO              Assemble and execute a single instruction
    GO              Execute VAX instructions
    IF              Run a command when an expression is true
    INCLUDE         Execute a text file of console commands
    INITIALIZE      Initialize the virtual VAX, or a disk-image container
    LIBRARY         Create, change, or list a macro or object library
    LINK            Link object modules into an executable image
    LOAD            Load a saved memory image
    MACRO           Assemble a MACRO-32 source file into an object module
    MOUNT           Attach a disk-image container file to a device
    PRINT           Print messages or expressions
    PURGE           Delete old versions of files on a mounted volume
    QUIT            Quit the virtual VAX
    RENAME          Rename or move files on a mounted volume
    RUN             Load and run a VAX/VMS image
    SAVE            Save the machine state
    SET             Set the state of the console
    SHOW            Display the state of the console
    TEST            Run the emulator's debugging sequences
    TIME            Run a command and report how long it took
    TYPE            Display a file on a mounted volume
    VMINIT          Initialize a VM map
    ZERO            Clear the virtual VAX's memory

    The commands that examine and control the machine itself (EXAMINE,
    DEPOSIT, STEP, breakpoints, the registers) are the debugger's; type
    DEBUG for its prompt, DBG>, and HELP there for its commands.

    Other topics:

    ABOUT           About govax
    CONFIG          govax's configuration settings, and how to set them
    KEYS            The control keys: Ctrl/C, Ctrl/Y, and Ctrl/Z
    PSL             The processor status longword and its fields
    XFC             govax's extended function codes
    

$DEBU
    The DEBUG command starts the debugger on the virtual VAX as it stands,
    with nothing running.  It is the way to examine memory and the
    registers, or set a breakpoint, before running a program (as typing
    Ctrl/Y and then DEBUG does for a VMS process).

    Syntax:

        DEBUG

    The prompt changes to DBG>.  The debugger is a separate command set,
    modeled on the VMS debugger: EXAMINE, DEPOSIT, STEP, GO, SET BREAK,
    SHOW CALLS, and so on.  The console's own commands (DIRECTORY, MACRO,
    LINK, RUN, ...) aren't available at DBG>.  EXIT returns to the console,
    leaving the machine as it is.  HELP at DBG> describes the debugger's
    commands.

    The debugger also starts by itself when a program stops: a GO or CALL
    that reaches a breakpoint, RUN/DEBUG, or an image linked /DEBUG.  A
    program that runs to its end returns to the console prompt, and the
    debugger doesn't appear.

$DO  
$.  
    The DO command (which can be abbreviated to a single period ".") takes
    a single VAX instruction, assembles it into scratch memory, and executes
    it.  This can be used to manipulate processor registers, etc.  The
    instruction is assembled into a page of system memory identified by the
    symbol CONSOLE$SCRATCH, normally set up by the VMINIT command.  The
    command is executed in single step mode, so only a single instruction
    will be executed even if it is a branch or causes an exception.

    Because the PC is restored to it's value before the command is given,
    it cannot be used to change the PC via  branch, etc.  Additionally,
    because it is a single command line, multi-line instructions like CASEx
    should not be used.

    Syntax:

        DO <statement>

    Parameter:

        <statement>   Any valid single line of assembler code.  Note that
                      this can include pseudo-ops if needed.  Note that
                      some statements make no sense in this context, such
                      as declaring a .vector or a .entry label.

    Examples:

        DO MOVL #33, R0     ; Sets R0 to the value 33
        .  INCL  R0         ; Increments the value in R0

$VMIN
$VM  

    The VMINIT command creates a functional virtual memory map that uses all
    of physical memory to create P0, P1, and S0 virtual address spaces.  It
    also initializes the required privileged registers and stack pointers to
    begin using it.  The command will display information about the physical
    and virtual address ranges for each region that it creates.
    
    Syntax:
    
        VMINIT  <region> = <pages> [ <region> = <pages>...]
    
    Parameters:
    
        <region>    This specifies a region that is to be explicitly sized.
                    If a region is explicitly sized, then the command is
                    specifying the number of pages of physical memory to
                    allocate to that region.  If a region is not explicitly
                    sized then unused physical memory is evenly divided
                    among the implicitly sized regions.
                    
                    A region can be one of P0, P1, or S0.
        
        <pages>     This specifies the number of pages to allocate to the
                    region.  This is in the current base, so be careful
                    to not assume decimal numbers, etc.
                    
                    Also note that the S0 space has a minimum size, based
                    on the sizes of the other regions.  This is because the
                    S0 region must contain the PFN database for P0 and P1
                    space, as well as minimal room for the privileged mode
                    stacks.  If you do not specify a large enough S0 space
                    (or there is not enough physical memory left to allocate
                    to it) then an error message is displayed.

    Qualifiers:

        /KSP=<pages>    The size of the kernel stack.  Default 4.
        /ESP=<pages>    The size of the executive stack.  Default 8.  Its
                        pages are writable only from executive or kernel
                        mode, with a no-access guard page below them.
        /SSP=<pages>    The size of the supervisor stack.  Default 8.  Its
                        pages are writable only from supervisor mode or a
                        more privileged mode, with a guard page below them.
        /ISP=<pages>    The size of the interrupt stack.  Default 4.

                    
$TEST

    The TEST statement executes special debugging sequences in the emulator.
    
    Syntax:
    
        TEST    <test>
    
    Parameter:
    
        <test>      The test number to execute.
        
                    11004   Toggle VM tracing
                    11003   Toggle translation buffer usage
                    11001   Generate instruction.h file

$ASM 
$ASSE
    The ASM command invokes the pseudo assembler.  This can be used to store
    instructions or data in memory.  It recognizes the standard VAX assembler
    syntax.  It can create a symbol table, including forward references.  It
    can also be used to set the attributes of the processor itself.

    The assembly continues until the end of the input file is read or a
    .END pseudo opcode is found.  See the help on ASM PSEUDO for more
    information on supported pseudo-opcodes, ASM LABELS for labels,
    local labels, and direct assignment, ASM ADDRESSING for how
    operands are encoded, and ASM EXPRESSIONS for expressions.

    A statement whose last character before any comment is a hyphen
    ("-") continues on the next line.
    
    Syntax:

        ASM  [<file>]

    Parameter:

        <file>       You can optionally specify a file on disk that is to
                     be read in and assembled.  If you do not specify a 
                     file then the console prompt changes to ASM> and the
                     assembler statements are entered from the keyboard.

$ASM ,LABE

    A statement may start with a label, which defines a symbol whose
    value is the current location:

        NAME:       An ordinary label.
        NAME::      A label that survives .CLEAR.
        10$:        A local label: decimal digits followed by "$".

    A local label is only known within its local label block, the code
    between two ordinary labels (.ENTRY, .SCOPE, .SHIM, .REGION, and
    .END also end a block).  The same local label, such as 10$, can be
    used again in each block.  Referring to a local label that isn't
    defined in the block before the block ends is an error.  Local
    labels are not kept in the console's symbol table.

    A symbol can also be given a value with a direct assignment:

        NAME = <expression>
        NAME == <expression>
        . = <expression>        Sets the current location.

    The expression may not contain forward references.

$ASM ,ADDR

    Operands use the VAX MACRO addressing mode syntax:

        Rn                  Register
        (Rn)                Register deferred
        (Rn)+  -(Rn)        Autoincrement, autodecrement
        @(Rn)+              Autoincrement deferred
        #value              Short literal, or immediate if it won't fit
        S^#value  I^#value  Short literal, immediate
        disp(Rn)  @disp(Rn) Displacement [deferred]
        B^ W^ L^disp(Rn)    Displacement of a given size
        address   @address  Relative [deferred]: PC plus a displacement
        B^ W^ L^address     Relative, with a displacement of a given size
        @#address           Absolute
        operand[Rx]         Indexed

    With no B^, W^, or L^ prefix, a displacement or relative address
    whose value is already known uses the smallest displacement that
    holds it.  A forward reference uses a longword displacement.  A
    relative operand is stored as the distance from the end of the
    operand to the address, so code that uses it must run at the
    address it was assembled for, or be moved as a whole.

$ASM ,EXPR

    Expressions follow VAX MACRO.  A number is decimal unless a radix
    operator says otherwise, and all binary operators have the same
    priority, applied left to right: 1+2*3 is 9, and 1+<2*3> is 7.

        <expression>        Grouping; ( ) also works
        + - * /             Arithmetic
        A@B                 Shift A left B bits (right if B is negative)
        A&B  A!B  A\B       Logical AND, inclusive OR, exclusive OR
        -A  +A              Negation
        ^C A                One's complement
        ^D ^X ^O ^B         Decimal, hexadecimal, octal, binary: ^X1F,
                            or ^X<10+20> for a whole expression
        ^A/text/            ASCII: up to four characters, any delimiter
        'text'              ASCII, as above
        ^M<R2,R3>           Register mask
        .                   The current location

    A number must be all digits of its radix: 0FF is an error, where
    ^X0FF is 255.  The console's SET RADIX does not affect the
    assembler, and disassembly shows hexadecimal values with ^X so
    that it can be assembled again.

    An expression may use a symbol that is defined later in the program
    (a forward reference) where it adds or subtracts that symbol, or
    multiplies it by a constant: SYM+8, B-A, 2*SYM, SYM-.  The value is
    completed when the symbol is defined.  Anything else done to a
    forward reference is an error.

$ASM ,PSEU

    The assembler supports a number of "pseudo-opcodes" which are really
    instructions to the assembler rather than actual VAX opcodes.  You can
    get help on each of the pseudo-opcodes.  To get help, use the command
    HELP ASM PSEUDO followed by the name of the pseudo-opcode.

    Note that in the actual assembler code, pseudo opcodes are identified
    by a leading "." character.  Do not specify this in the help command.

        ALIGN
        ASCIC
        ASCID
        ASCII
        ASCIZ
        BASE
        BLKB
        BLKD
        BLKF
        BLKL
        BLKW
        BYTE
        CASE
        CLEAR
        CONSOLE
        D_FLOAT
        END
        ENDM
        ENDR
        ENTRY
        ERROR
        F_FLOAT
        G_FLOATING
        H_FLOATING
        IF
        IIF
        INCLUDE
        IRP
        IRPC
        LONG
        MACRO
        MDELETE
        MEXIT
        MODE
        NARG
        NCHR
        NTYPE
        OCTA
        PACKED
        PRINT
        PTE
        QUAD
        REGION
        REPEAT
        SCB
        SET
        SHIM
        WARN
        WORD
        VECTOR
        
$ASM ,PSEU,VECT

    The VECTOR directive starts assembling code that is stored in the
    SCB vector.  The address of the code following the directive is
    stored in the SCB slot.

    Syntax:

        .VECTOR  <slot-number> [, <stack-name> ]

    Parameter:

        <slot-number>   This is the number of the slot that will be
                        filled with the address of the following
                        instructions.  The address is aligned on an
                        8-byte boundary automatically.  The slot
                        number is usually expressed symbolically,
                        such as with EXC$ARITH for the arithmentic
                        trap slot.

        <stack-name>    This can be either ISP or KSP.  If omitted,
                        then KSP is assumed.  This indicates if the
                        fault runs on the interrupt stack (with VM
                        turned off) or not.

$ASM ,PSEU,CLEA

    The CLEAR directive will clear all symbols from the user symbol table,
    or a specific symbol.  If no name is given then all symbols will be
    cleared.

    Syntax:

        .CLEAR   [ <symbol-name> ]

$ASM ,PSEU,SET 

    The SET directive sets a symbol in the symbol table.  This can often
    be used in conjunction with expressions containing the "." operator
    which means the current location being assembled into.

    Syntax:

        .SET   <symbol-name> <value>

    Parameters:

        <symbol-name>     The name of the symbol to create or set.

        <value>           Any valid expression.

$ASM ,PSEU,SCB 

    The SCB directive allows you to define a system control block address
    for catching system exceptions.  These are stored in the SCB by the
    assembler, using the current value of the SCBB base register.

    Syntax:

        .SCB  <exception>, <address> [, <stack>]


    Parameters:

        <exception>     This is the exception code.  This must be a
                        multiple of 4.  Usually, it is defined using
                        one of the predefined symbols.  For example,
                        use the symbol EXC$ACCVIO to indicate the
                        Access Violation exception handler slot in 
                        the SCB.

        <address>       This is the address of the exception handler.
                        It must be a quadword aligned address.

        <stack>         If given, this may be ISP or KSP to indicate
                        which stack the handler is to run on.  If
                        omitted, then the Kernel Stack Pointer is
                        assumed.

$ASM ,PSEU,ALIG

    The ALIGN directive advances the current location if needed
    to ensure that it is an even multiple of the alignment value.
    For example, a system exception handler must start on a quad-
    word boundary, so an .ALIGN 8 can be used to ensure that the
    following instruction is sure to have the correct starting
    address.  Note that while the current location may be changed
    by this directive, nothing is written into the memory locations
    that are skipped over.

    Syntax:

        .ALIGN  <expression>

    Parameter:

        <expression>     An integer expression, indicating the
                         multiple that the current location must
                         match.  Typically, values of 2, 4, or 8
                         are most often used for word, longword,
                         and quadword boundaries.

$ASM ,PSEU,REGI

    The REGION pseudo opcode switches between S0 and P0 address 
    spaces.  The assembler maintains two "current locations",
    one for each of the code regions.  The .BASE directive can
    be used to set the current location for the current region.
    Using the REGION directive selects which current locations
    is to be used.

    After initialization, the P0 base starts at address 00000200
    and the S0 region starts at the first page following the SCB
    and page tables.

    Syntax:

        .REGION  <region>

    Parameter:

        <region>    The region to assemble instructions into. 
                    This can be P0, 0, or PROCESS for the P0
                    region, or S0, 2, or SYSTEM for the S0
                    region.

$ASM ,PSEU,PRIN

    The PRINT opcode is used to display messages during assembly of
    a source file, such as version or copyright information.  This is
    equivalent to the PRINT command and has the same parameters.

    Syntax:

        .PRINT    <expression>  [, <expression> ]

$ASM ,PSEU,BASE

    The BASE opcode is used to set the address (physical or virtual,
    depending on the MAPEN register setting) to begin storing the 
    next instructions or data.  This is equivalent to the console
    command SET BASE.

    Syntax:

        .BASE    <expression>

    Parameter:

        <expression>     Any valid integer expression.  This defines
                         the location where the next instruction or
                         data will be written.

$ASM ,PSEU,BYTE
$ASM ,PSEU,WORD
$ASM ,PSEU,LONG
$ASM ,PSEU,QUAD
$ASM ,PSEU,OCTA

    The BYTE, WORD, LONG, QUAD, and OCTA pseudo-opcodes store specific
    given values of the indicated size in memory: 1, 2, 4, 8, or 16
    bytes.

    Syntax:

        .BYTE    <data> [, <data> ]

        .WORD    <data> [, <data> ]

        .LONG    <data> [, <data> ]

        .QUAD    <data> [, <data> ]

        .OCTA    <data> [, <data> ]


     Parameter:

        <data>     The data value(s) to be stored.  If more than one
                   is given, they must be separated by commas.  The
                   data is stored in memory in the order given.  A
                   BYTE or WORD value may be given signed (-1) or
                   unsigned (255).  A QUAD or OCTA value that is a
                   single number, such as ^X0123456789ABCDEF, is
                   stored in full.  Any other expression is computed
                   as a longword and sign-extended, as eVAX did; in
                   MACRO-32 source it is zero-extended, and a negative
                   number such as -1 is an error, as with VAX MACRO.

$ASM ,PSEU,F_FL
$ASM ,PSEU,D_FL
$ASM ,PSEU,G_FL
$ASM ,PSEU,H_FL

    The F_FLOAT, D_FLOAT, G_FLOATING, and H_FLOATING pseudo-opcodes
    (also F_FLOATING, D_FLOATING, FLOAT, and DOUBLE) store floating
    values in the VAX's four formats: 4, 8, 8, or 16 bytes.

    Syntax:

        .F_FLOAT     <value> [, <value> ]

        .D_FLOAT     <value> [, <value> ]

        .G_FLOATING  <value> [, <value> ]

        .H_FLOATING  <value> [, <value> ]

     Parameter:

        <value>    A decimal number, such as 1.5, -0.1, or 6.02E23.
                   Each is rounded once, from its decimal value, to
                   the format's precision (half away from zero, as
                   the VAX rounds).  A value too large for the format
                   is an error; one too small is stored as zero.

$ASM ,PSEU,PACK

    The PACKED pseudo-opcode stores a packed decimal string: the
    digits two to a byte, most significant first, and the sign in the
    last byte's low four bits (hex C for plus, D for minus).  An even
    number of digits gets a zero first half-byte.  The decimal string
    instructions (MOVP, ADDP4, CVTPL, EDITPC, ...) work on these.

    Syntax:

        .PACKED  <decimal-string> [, <symbol> ]

     Parameter:

        <decimal-string>  A decimal number of 0 to 31 digits, with an
                          optional sign: -12, +500, 0.

        <symbol>          If given, it is set to the number of digits
                          (not counting the sign), the length the
                          decimal instructions take.

$ASM ,PSEU,ENTR

    The ENTRY opcode defines a procedure entry point.  It can be followed
    by an optional register mask specification.  The symbol is stored as
    an entry mask label, and a mask word is generated.

    Syntax:

        .ENTRY    <name> [, <mask> ]

    Parameter:

        <name>    The required name of the entry point.  This may be
                  used as a label in a CALLS or CALLG instruction, or
                  as the entry for a CALL console command.

        <mask>    If specified, this determines the register mask to
                  associate with the procedure.  If ommitted, then a
                  mask of zero (no saved registers) is assumed.  The
                  mask is usually specified as ^m<r2,r3> syntax.

$ASM ,PSEU,END 

    The END opcode terminates the assembly.  If assembling from the
    console terminal, it returns the prompt to the normal VAX> prompt.

    Syntax:

        .END    [<entry>]

    Parameter:

        <entry>        If specified, it indicates the starting entry
                       point for execution.  The symbol __ENTRY is set
                       to this value (which can be any valid address
                       expression).  A CALL instruction is generated to
                       this address to start the program.

                       If you do not want to auto-execute an assembled
                       program, do not include an <entry> value on the
                       END statement.

$ASM ,PSEU,BLKB
$ASM ,PSEU,BLKW
$ASM ,PSEU,BLKL

    The block storage opcodes BLKB, BLKL, and BLKW create a block of
    zeros of byte, longword, or word length.

    Syntax:

        .BLKB      <count>        Creates <count> bytes of zeros.

        .BLKW      <count>        Creates <count> 16-bit words of zeros.

        .BLKL      <count>        Creates <count> 32-bit longwords of 
                                  zeros.

    Parameter:

        <count>        The number of elements to write to the storage.
                       The actual number of bytes is <count> * <size>
                       where <size> is specified by the opcode (byte,
                       word, or longword).

$ASM ,PSEU,ASCI

    The character string storage pseudo-opcodes ASCIC, ASCID, ASCII, and
    ASCIZ all store a string in memory.  The differences are in the format
    of the string storage.

    Syntax:

        .ASCIC     "<string>"      Stores a counted ASCII string.  The
                                   storage contains a byte that holds
                                   the length of <string> (at most 255),
                                   followed by the bytes of the string.

        .ASCID     "<string>"      Stores a string descriptor.  The first
                                   eight bytes are a standard VMS string
                                   descriptor, followed by the actual bytes
                                   of the string.  The descriptor is
                                   initialized with the length and address
                                   of the string data.

        .ASCII     "<string>"      The bytes of the string data are stored
                                   in memory.

        .ASCIZ     "<string>"      A null terminated string is stored.  The
                                   bytes of <string> are written, followed
                                   by an additional byte containing a zero.

    As in VAX MACRO, the string can be delimited by any printing
    character except blank, "=", ";", or "<", as long as it does not
    appear in the string, and lowercase letters are kept.  Between or
    after delimited strings, an expression in angle brackets stores one
    byte, for characters such as a line feed:

        CR = ^D13
        LF = ^D10
        .ASCII    /Line one/<CR><LF>
        .ASCIZ    "It's done."<LF>

    A backslash is an ordinary character; there are no escapes like \n.
    The descriptor .ASCID stores has class S and type T (information
    word 010E).

$ASM ,PSEU,IF  
$ASM ,PSEU,IIF 
$ASM ,PSEU,ENDC
$ASM ,PSEU,IFF 
$ASM ,PSEU,IFT 
$ASM ,PSEU,IFTF
$ASM ,PSEU,IF_F
$ASM ,PSEU,IF_T

    Conditional assembly, as in VAX MACRO.  The statements between .IF
    and its .ENDC are assembled only if the condition is met.  Blocks
    can be nested up to 31 deep.  Statements left out, including any
    labels on them, are not assembled at all.

    Syntax:

        .IF      <condition> <argument>
        ...
        .ENDC

        .IIF     <condition> <argument>, <statement>

    Within an .IF block, these switch the rest of the block on or off:

        .IF_FALSE  (.IFF)       Assemble if the condition was not met
        .IF_TRUE   (.IFT)       Assemble if the condition was met
        .IF_TRUE_FALSE (.IFTF)  Assemble either way

    Conditions (long and short forms), which may be followed by a comma:

        EQUAL  EQ     NOT_EQUAL      NE     <expression> is 0 / not 0
        GREATER  GT   LESS_EQUAL     LE     <expression> is > 0 / <= 0
        LESS_THAN LT  GREATER_EQUAL  GE     <expression> is < 0 / >= 0
        DEFINED  DF   NOT_DEFINED    NDF    <symbol> is defined / not;
                                            A&B needs both, A!B either
        BLANK  B      NOT_BLANK      NB     <text> is blank / not
        IDENTICAL IDN DIFFERENT      DIF    <text>,<text> are the same /
                                            differ

    An expression argument may not use a symbol defined later in the
    program.

    Example:

        .IIF     NOT_DEFINED SS$_NORMAL, .INCLUDE "ssdef.asm"

        .IF      DF DEBUG
        MOVL     #1, R0
        .IF_FALSE
        CLRL     R0
        .ENDC

$ASM ,PSEU,MACR
$ASM ,PSEU,ENDM
$ASM ,PSEU,MDEL
$ASM ,PSEU,MEXI
$ASM ,PSEU,NARG
$ASM ,PSEU,NCHR
$ASM ,PSEU,NTYP

    Macros, as in VAX MACRO.  A macro definition is a named block of
    source; a statement naming the macro (a macro call) assembles the
    block with the call's arguments put in place of the formal ones.
    Macros are tried before instructions, so a macro can replace one.

    Syntax:

        .MACRO   <name> [<formal>[=<default>],...]
        ...
        .ENDM    [<name>]

        <name>   [<argument>,...]

    A formal argument named ?<name> gets a created local label (30000$,
    30001$, ...) when the call gives no argument for it.  A call's
    arguments are positional, or <formal>=<value> in any order; <...>
    or ^x...x encloses one holding commas or blanks, and \<symbol>
    passes a symbol's value in decimal.  An argument is put in place
    everywhere in the block, strings and comments included; an
    apostrophe next to a formal argument's name joins it to the text
    around it (PREFIX'_'N).  Arguments keep their case.

        .MEXIT                  Ends the macro's expansion early
        .MDELETE <name>,...     Deletes macro definitions
        .NARG    <symbol>       The number of positional arguments given
        .NCHR    <symbol>,<text>  The number of characters in <text>
        .NTYPE   <symbol>,<operand>  The addressing mode of <operand>

    In a macro's lines, %LENGTH(<text>), %LOCATE(<text1>,<text2>[,<start>]),
    and %EXTRACT(<start>,<length>,<text>) are replaced by their results.

    The MACRO command also finds macros in macro libraries (see HELP
    MACRO); ASM uses only the macros the source defines.

    Example:

        .MACRO   MAX A, B, DEST, ?SKIP
        MOVL     A, DEST
        CMPL     B, DEST
        BLEQ     SKIP
        MOVL     B, DEST
SKIP:
        .ENDM    MAX

        MAX      R1, R2, R0

$ASM ,PSEU,REPE
$ASM ,PSEU,REPT
$ASM ,PSEU,IRP 
$ASM ,PSEU,IRPC
$ASM ,PSEU,ENDR

    Repeat blocks, as in VAX MACRO: the statements up to .ENDR are
    assembled several times.

    Syntax:

        .REPEAT  <count>        (or .REPT)  <count> times
        .IRP     <symbol>,<<argument>,...>  once for each argument, with
                                            <symbol> replaced by it
        .IRPC    <symbol>,<<text>>          once for each character
        ...
        .ENDR

    .MEXIT in a repeat block ends it, skipping the repetitions left.

    Example:

        .IRP     REG, <R2, R3, R4>
        CLRL     REG
        .ENDR

$ASM ,PSEU,ERRO
$ASM ,PSEU,WARN

    .ERROR and .WARN report a message of the program's own: .ERROR as an
    assembly error, and .WARN as a warning.  The message is the value of
    the expression, if one is given and it isn't zero, then the
    statement's comment.  They're most useful in a macro, in a
    conditional block, to check its arguments.

    Syntax:

        .ERROR   [<expression>] ;<comment>
        .WARN    [<expression>] ;<comment>

    Example:

        .IIF     BLANK <ARG>, .ERROR ; ARG is required

    In the MACRO command, .PRINT displays its comment the same way; the
    ASM command's .PRINT is its own (see HELP ASM PSEUDO PRINT).

$ASM ,PSEU,PTE 

    The .PTE pseudo operator creates a page table entry longword at the current
    location in memory.

$ASM ,PSEU,CASE

    The .CASE pseudo operator is used to construct the branch table that
    follows a CASEB, CASEW, or CASEL instruction.  The branch table must
    be stored in memory immediately following the CASEx instruction.  Each
    element of the branch table is a displacement address of where the
    CASEx will transfer to based on the selector operand.

    This is difficult to code without the .CASE since the table must be
    a set of word displacements from the start of the table.  With the
    .CASE pseudo operator you can simply express it as a list of
    labeled destinations that can be forward references if needed.

    Syntax:

        CASEx selector.rx, base.rx, limit.rx

        .CASE    dest1 [, dest2...]

    Parameters:

        The above syntax specifies that a CASEx opcode must precede the
        .CASE table for it to be meaningful.  This can be followed by one
        or more .CASE pseudo opcodes.  If more than one item is to be
        given for each .CASE then they must be separated by commas.

        Note that an error occurs if the displacement between the label
        and the address of the first .CASE entry cannot be expressed as
        a signed 16-bit word.

    Example:

                  CASEB     (R0), @#BASE_VALUE, #2
                  .CASE     RED
                  .CASE     BLUE
                  .CASE     GREEN
                  MOVL      #ERR_CODE, R0
                  ...
 
        RED:      ADDL2   R2, R3
                  ... 


        In this example, there is a case table of three items.  Note
        that the limit value is zero-based, so for a table of three
        items the range is 0-2 and the limit is 2.  Each .CASE specifies
        the branch destinations in order.  One of these will be chosen
        by the VAX based on the selector in R0, less the base value.
        Note that if the selector value is outside the range allowed
        by the base value and limit, then control resumes after the
        .CASE block with the MOVL of the error code.

        It is an undetected programmer error to specify a .CASE table
        that is not _exactly_ as long as the limit-1 specifies!


$PRIN

    The PRINT command can be used to print messages or expressions.  The 
    text parts of a PRINT command are displayed just as they are shown
    in the quotation marks.  Numeric expressions can be PRINTED as well,
    and are displayed in the current default radix.  If the radix is
    decimal, then they are printed as decimal numbers without leading
    spaces or zeroes.  If the radix is hexadecimal, the values are
    printed as 8-byte hexadecimal values.

    Syntax:

        PRINT <expression> [, <expression>...]

    Parameters:

        <expression>    One or more expressions or quoted string values,
                        separated by commas.

$TIME

    The TIME command runs another console command and reports how long it
    took.  With no command, it prints the time of day.

    Syntax:

        TIME [<command>]

    Parameter:

        <command>       Any console command.

    Example:

        TIME DIRECTORY

$IF

    The IF command runs a command only when an expression is true (nonzero).
    It is used in command files (see INCLUDE).

    Syntax:

        IF <expression> [THEN] <command>

    Parameters:

        <expression>    An expression, in the console's radix.  Functions
                        such as DEFINED("name") may be used in it.

        <command>       Any console command, run as a line of its own when
                        the expression is nonzero.

    Example:

        IF DEFINED("CONSOLE$ARG_FILE") THEN SET NOVERBOSE

$BOOT
$ROM

    BOOT and ROM are reserved for booting the virtual VAX from a device and
    from its console ROM.  They are not implemented yet, and say so when
    typed.  LOAD/ROM and SAVE/ROM, which read and write the ROM's contents,
    are (see HELP LOAD and HELP SAVE).

$AUTH
$ABOU

