    via Claude Code.
    
$COMM,PARA

    The descriptions of the commands include various parameter types.  These
    are denoted by a name in angle brackets.  For example, <address> means
    that the parameter is to be an address value.
    
    This help entry describes the command parameter types shown in the other
    help entries.
    
    <address>           This must be a valid address value.  If virtual memory
                        is enabled in the VAX, then it is a virtual address
                        and will be translated accordingly.  If virtual memory
                        is not enabled, it is interpreted as a physical 
                        address.
                        
                        It may be a numeric value, either in the default
                        radix (see the SET RADIX command) or with an explicit
                        radix specifier prefix like D^ for decimal or X^ for
                        hexadecimal. 
                        
                        It can also be a symbolic value, which is a name 
                        optionally enclosed in single quotes.  The symbolic 
                        value is replaced with it's numeric value and then 
                        used.
    
    <value>             A numeric value.  By default this is a 32-bit value,
                        since that is the natural data size for VAX.  It can
                        be in the default radix (see the SET RADIX command)
                        or can have an explicity radix specifier prefix of
                        D^ for a decimal value or X^ for a hexadecimal value.

    Note that in the case of both <value> and <address> you can construct
    an integer expression to represent the value.  You can use +, -, *, and
    / operators, and parenthesis.  Note that an expression that contains a
    symbol can only use symbols that are already defined.  (An assembler
    program can use forward references; see ASM EXPRESSIONS.)

$FUNCTIONS

    Any place an arithmetic expression can be used, functions can be used as
    replacements for numeric arguments.  Functions are built-in to the expression
    handler for the console.  They may accept between zero and four arguments
    depending on the function.  The result is always numeric.
    
    BYTE( addr )            Returns the integer byte located at the given address.
  
    DEFINED( "string" )     Returns 0 if the string is not the name of a symbol.
                            Returns 1 if the string is a user symbol name.
                            Returns 2 if the string is a system symbol name.
    
    LONG( addr )            Returns the integer longword located at the given address.
      
    PMEMSIZE()              Returns the number of bytes of physical memory in
                            the virtual VAX.
    
    UBYTE( addr )           Returns the unsigned byte located at the given address.
    
    ULONG( addr )           Returns the unsigned long located at the given address.
    
    UWORD( addr )           Returns the unsigned word located at the given address.
    
    WORD( addr )            Returns the integer word located at the given address.
    
    FILE_EXISTS( "name" )   Returns 1 if the named file exists, else zero
    
    VMVALID()               Returns 1 if console VM is enabled, else zero
    
    MKVALID()               Returns 1 if console microkernel is enabled, else zero.
$PSL 

    The PSL is a 32-bit integer value stored in the processor.  The SHOW PSL
    command can be used to display the PSL as an integer, and also as the
    individual fields of the PSL.  These fields are:
    
    C       carry bit
    V       overflow bit
    Z       zero value
    N       negative value
    T       trace pending
    IV      integer overflow
    FU      floating point underflow
    DV      divide-by-zero
    IPL     current interrupt priority level
    MOD     current mode (user, kernel, supervisor, executive)

$XFC 

    The XFC (extended function code) opcode is supplied by Digital as part
    of the VAX architecture to allow extensions to the instruction set.
    The instruction is always followed by a single immediate byte that
    contains the function operation to perform.

    The govax emulator defines several XFC operations that can be used in
    assembler programs.  The operations can be identified by symbolic
    names as shown below.

    XFC  #XFC$CONSOLE_WRITE     ; 01

    The low-order byte of R0 is written to the console terminal.  Note 
    that this is also possible by correctly constructing a write to the
    TXDB privileged register if the process is in kernel mode.


    XFC  #XFC$CONSOLE_READ      ; 02

    A single character is read from the console terminal, and is stored
    in the low-order byte of R0.  The rest of R0 is undisturbed.


    XFC  #XFC$CONSOLE_CMD       ; 03

    The R0 register must point to a counted ASCII string that contains
    a single govax console command.  The command is executed, and the
    return code is stored back in R0, replacing it's contents.

    The command text *must* be in upper-case or it will not be 
    recognized.  This limitation will be removed in a future release.


    XFC  #XFC$VM                ; 7F

    The value in R0 as assumed to be a virtual address.  The virtual
    address is translated (if possible) to a physical address.  This
    occurs regardless of the state of the MAPEN register, so it assumes
    that page tables are correctly set up (see the VMINIT console command).

    The resulting physical address is written to R0.  If a TNV fault
    occurs because the page is not mapped to physical memory, then the
    V bit of the PSL is set and the result in R0 is undefined.


$MOUN

    The MOUNT command attaches a disk-image container file to a device
    name, making a real ODS-2 volume available for RMS file access by a
    program: the SYS$CREATE, SYS$OPEN, SYS$CLOSE, SYS$CONNECT, SYS$GET,
    SYS$PUT, SYS$RENAME, SYS$PARSE, SYS$SEARCH, and SYS$DISPLAY services,
    with name blocks (NAM) and the XABDAT, XABRDT, XABFHC, XABPRO, XABALL,
    and XABSUM extended attribute blocks.
    If the named device is not already known to the console, MOUNT creates
    it automatically as a disk-class device.

    Mounting a device that already has a container mounted on it is an
    error; use DISMOUNT first if you want to attach a different container
    to the same device name.

    Syntax:

        MOUNT [/WRITE] [/NOWRITE] <device> <file>

    Parameters:

        <device>        The device name to attach the container to, such
                        as DUA0.  A trailing colon is optional.  A logical
                        name is translated to the device it names; a
                        leading underscore (_DUA0:) prevents that.

        <file>          The path to the disk-image container file on the
                        native file system.  If the native file system is
                        case sensitive, or the name contains embedded
                        blanks, it should be enclosed in quotes to
                        preserve case and spacing.

    Qualifiers:

        /WRITE          Mounts the volume for both reading and writing.
                        This is the default.

        /NOWRITE        Mounts the volume read-only.  Any attempt to
                        create or write a file on the volume fails.

    Examples:

        MOUNT DUA0 disk1.dsk
        %MOUNT-I-MOUNTED, USERDISK mounted on _DUA0:

        MOUNT/NOWRITE DUA1 "system disk.dsk"

    MOUNT reports the volume's label and the device it was mounted on,
    as above, unless SET NOVERBOSE has turned the console's messages off.

    As on VMS, MOUNT defines the logical name DISK$label (for example
    DISK$USERDISK for a volume labeled USERDISK) in the system table, so
    files on the volume can be named by label: DISK$USERDISK:[DIR]FILE.
    DISMOUNT deletes it again.

    See also the help for DISMOUNT and SHOW DEVICE.

$DISM

    The DISMOUNT command detaches the disk-image container currently
    mounted on a device, flushing any pending writes and closing the
    underlying container file.  The device itself remains known to the
    console; only its mounted volume is removed.  A later MOUNT command
    can attach a different container to the same device name.

    Dismounting a device with nothing currently mounted on it is an error.

    Syntax:

        DISMOUNT <device>

    Parameter:

        <device>        The device name to detach, such as DUA0.  A
                        trailing colon is optional.  A logical name, such
                        as the DISK$label name MOUNT defined, is
                        translated first.

    Example:

        DISMOUNT DUA0

    See also the help for MOUNT and SHOW DEVICE.

$DIRE
$DIR

    The DIRECTORY command lists files on a mounted ODS-2 volume.

    Syntax:

        DIRECTORY [/FULL] [/FILE] [/SIZE] [/DATE] [/OWNER] [/PROTECTION]
                  [/VERSIONS=n] [file-spec]

    Parameter:

        file-spec       The file specification to list, which may include
                        wildcards (e.g. DUA0:[MYDIR]*.TXT;*).  Any part of
                        the specification left out is filled in from the
                        current default device/directory (see SET
                        DEFAULT).  If omitted entirely, lists "*.*;*" --
                        every file, every version, in the current default
                        directory.

    Qualifiers:

        /FULL           Includes everything the qualifiers below show,
                        and each file's record format.

        /FILE           Includes each file's file ID.

        /SIZE           Includes each file's size, in blocks.

        /DATE           Includes each file's date.

        /OWNER          Includes each file's owner UIC, [group,member].

        /PROTECTION     Includes each file's protection: the access
                        granted to system, owner, group, and world, in
                        that order, as in (RWED,RWED,RE,).

        /VERSIONS=n     Lists only the n most recent versions of each
                        file: /VERSIONS=1 shows just the newest.  The
                        value is required and must be at least 1.

    Examples:

        DIRECTORY

        DIRECTORY/VERSIONS=2 *.TXT;*

        DIRECTORY/SIZE *.TXT;*

        DIR DUA0:[MYDIR]

    See also the help for SET DEFAULT, SHOW DEFAULT, TYPE, and MOUNT.

$DELE
$DEL

    The DELETE command removes a file from a mounted ODS-2 volume and
    reclaims its storage.

    A DELETE file specification must name a specific version -- either an
    explicit version number (;3) or all versions (;*).  Unlike DIRECTORY,
    TYPE, and COPY, DELETE never defaults an omitted version to "the
    newest one"; this matches real VMS DCL's own DELETE, which requires an
    explicit version to guard against accidentally deleting the wrong
    copy of a file.

    Syntax:

        DELETE <file-spec>;<version>

    Parameter:

        <file-spec>;<version>
                        The file, and version, to delete.  <file-spec> may
                        include wildcards in its name/type; any part left
                        out is filled in from the current default device/
                        directory (see SET DEFAULT).

    Examples:

        DELETE FOO.TXT;1

        DELETE OLDFILE.DAT;*

        DEL *.TMP;1

    See also the help for PURGE, DIRECTORY, and SET DEFAULT.

$PURG

    The PURGE command trims old versions of a file down to a specified
    number of surviving versions, reclaiming the storage of each version
    removed.  A file spec matching no files at all is not an error.

    Syntax:

        PURGE [/LIMIT=<n>] [file-spec]

    Parameter:

        file-spec       The file, or files (wildcards allowed), to purge.
                        Any version selector typed as part of file-spec is
                        ignored -- PURGE always considers every surviving
                        version of a matched name.  Any part left out is
                        filled in from the current default device/
                        directory (see SET DEFAULT).  If omitted entirely,
                        defaults to "*.*" -- every name in the current
                        default directory.

    Qualifier:

        /LIMIT=<n>      The number of most-recent versions to keep for
                        each matched name.  Defaults to 1 (keep only the
                        newest version) if omitted.

    Examples:

        PURGE FOO.TXT

        PURGE/LIMIT=2

    See also the help for DELETE, DIRECTORY, and SET DEFAULT.

$RENA
$REN 

    The RENAME command changes the name of one or more files on a mounted
    ODS-2 volume, or moves them to another directory, as VMS DCL's RENAME
    does.  The files' contents are not copied.  A file can't be renamed
    to another device: the old and new names must be on the same volume.

    Syntax:

        RENAME [/LOG] [/[NO]NEW_VERSION] <input>[,<input>...] <output>

    Parameters:

        <input>         The file or files to rename.  Wildcards are
                        allowed.  With no version, each name's highest
                        version is renamed.  The device and directory of
                        one input carry over to the next as its defaults,
                        and any part left out of the first is filled in
                        from the current default device/directory (see
                        SET DEFAULT).

        <output>        The new name.  Any part left out -- device,
                        directory, name, or type -- is the input file's
                        own, and a name, type, or version written as "*"
                        is the input file's too, so one output serves
                        several files (RENAME *.TXT *.OLD).  No other
                        wildcards are allowed.  With no output version, a
                        file keeps its version if the input named one (or
                        ;*); otherwise it gets the next version of its new
                        name.  An existing name and version is never
                        replaced.

    Qualifiers:

        /LOG            Reports each file renamed.

        /NEW_VERSION    With no versions given, gives each file the next
        /NONEW_VERSION  version of its new name (the default).  With
                        /NONEW_VERSION, each file keeps its version.

    A directory file (NAME.DIR;1) can be renamed or moved like any other
    file, taking everything in it along, but not into itself or one of
    its own subdirectories.  A file that can't be renamed is reported and
    the rest are still renamed.

    Examples:

        RENAME FEES.DAT;4 [SAVETEXT]RECORDS.DAT

        RENAME/LOG *.TXT *.OLD

        RENAME [A]X.TXT,Y.TXT [B]

        REN/NONEW_VERSION REPORT.LIS SUMMARY

    See also the help for COPY, DELETE, DIRECTORY, and SET DEFAULT.

$TYPE
$TYP

    The TYPE command writes one file's entire content to the console as
    text, expanding VFC/Fixed/Variable/Stream record formats into
    readable lines the same way COPY's own text-mode copying does.

    TYPE requires its file specification to match exactly one file --
    unlike DIRECTORY, it does not accept a wildcard that could match more
    than one file.

    Syntax:

        TYPE [/PAGE] <file-spec>

    Parameter:

        <file-spec>     The file to display.  An omitted version defaults
                        to the highest surviving version.  Any part left
                        out is filled in from the current default device/
                        directory (see SET DEFAULT).

    Qualifier:

        /PAGE           Display the file a screenful at a time.  After
                        each screen, TYPE prompts "Press RETURN to
                        continue": RETURN shows the next screen, and any
                        text typed before RETURN ends the display.  The
                        screen's size is the terminal's; when that can't
                        be read, the vax.console.lines setting gives its
                        height (default 24 lines).

    Examples:

        TYPE FOO.TXT

        TYPE/PAGE DUA0:[MYDIR]README.TXT;2

    See also the help for COPY, DIRECTORY, and SET DEFAULT.

$COPY

    The COPY command copies a file between a mounted ODS-2 volume and the
    host file system, or between two locations on a mounted volume.  The
    direction of each half of the copy is determined independently: a
    SOURCE or DESTINATION naming a location on a mounted volume (e.g.
    DUA0:[DIR]NAME.TYPE) is used as-is, while the /HOST qualifier,
    attached to either parameter, tells COPY to instead treat that
    parameter as an ordinary host file system path.

    Syntax:

        COPY <source>[/HOST] <destination>[/HOST] [qualifiers...]

    Parameters:

        <source>        The file to copy from.  With /HOST attached, this
                        is a host file system path; otherwise it names a
                        file (or, when <destination> names a directory,
                        several wildcard-matched files) on a mounted
                        volume.

        <destination>   The file, or directory, to copy to.  With /HOST
                        attached, this is a host file system path;
                        otherwise it names a file, or directory, on a
                        mounted volume.  A destination naming no file of
                        its own (a bare directory, container or host)
                        inherits each copied source file's own name and
                        type.

    Object modules (.OBJ files) are always copied as records, whatever
    the qualifiers.  On the host, an object module is kept in the same
    layout ODS-2 uses on disk for variable-length records (each record
    a 2-byte length, then its bytes, padded to an even length), so its
    record boundaries survive a copy to the host and back.  A host .OBJ
    copied onto a volume becomes a variable-length record file with the
    attributes VAX MACRO gives an object module.

    Qualifiers:

        /BINARY         Copies exact bytes rather than rendering the file
                        as text -- no record-format interpretation, no
                        line-ending translation.  A copy between two
                        volume locations keeps the source's record
                        format and attributes.

        /QUIET          Suppresses the normal %COPY-S-COPIED confirmation
                        line.

        /VERBOSE        Prints an additional %COPY-I-COPYING line before
                        each file is copied.

        /TEST           Shows what would be copied without actually
                        copying anything.

        /TIME           (Container-to-host copies only.)  Preserves the
                        source file's own VMS revision date as the copied
                        host file's modification time.

        /IGNORE         (Copies to a host destination only.)  Recovers
                        from a corrupt record partway through a file by
                        restarting the copy as a raw byte-for-byte copy,
                        rather than failing outright.

        /DIRS           (Copies to a host destination only.)  Materializes
                        a matched directory entry as an empty host
                        directory, and mirrors each matched file's own
                        volume subdirectory path under the destination
                        directory, instead of flattening every match into
                        one destination directory.

        /STREAM         (Copies to a host destination only.)  Copies a
                        Stream-format source file's exact bytes rather
                        than normalizing its line endings.

        /VFC            Accepted for command-line compatibility with the
                        ods2 utility's own COPY command.  Not otherwise
                        meaningful here: this project's text-mode copy
                        already always expands a VFC file's carriage
                        control, so there is no "un-interpreted" mode for
                        this qualifier to select.

        /CRLF           (Copies to a host destination only.)  Ends each
                        copied text line with CRLF instead of the default
                        LF.

        /LF             (Copies to a host destination only.)  Ends each
                        copied text line with LF.  This is the default;
                        /CRLF and /LF cannot both be given.

    Examples:

        COPY FOO.TXT BAR.TXT

        COPY foo.txt/HOST BAR.TXT

        COPY FOO.TXT bar.txt/HOST

        COPY "/Users/tom/foo.txt" /HOST BAR.TXT

    See also the help for TYPE, DIRECTORY, MOUNT, and SET DEFAULT.

$MACR

    The MACRO command assembles a VAX MACRO-32 source file and writes a
    VAX/VMS object module (.OBJ), the same kind of file VMS's own MACRO
    writes for its linker.

    Syntax:

        MACRO <source>[/HOST] [/OBJECT[=<object>] | /NOOBJECT]
              [/LIST[=<listing>] | /NOLIST] [/LIBRARY=(<library>[,...])]
              [/SHOW=(<option>[,...]) | /NOSHOW=(<option>[,...])]
              [/CROSS_REFERENCE[=(<option>[,...])] | /NOCROSS_REFERENCE]
              [/DEBUG[=(<option>[,...])] | /NODEBUG]
              [/ENABLE=(<function>[,...])] [/DISABLE=(<function>[,...])]

    Parameter:

        <source>        The source file.  Without a file type it is .MAR.
                        The source can be a host file or a file on a
                        mounted volume:

                          - with /HOST attached, it is a host path;
                          - a name with a directory in brackets
                            ([DIR]HELLO.MAR) or a device or logical name
                            of two or more characters (DUA0:HELLO.MAR,
                            SRC:HELLO.MAR) is on a mounted volume;
                          - a name with "/" or "\" (or a Windows drive
                            letter) is a host path;
                          - any other name (HELLO.MAR) is in the current
                            default directory when SET DEFAULT names a
                            mounted volume, and a host file otherwise.

                        Quote a host path to keep its case; unquoted
                        names are uppercased, as all DCL input is.

    Qualifiers:

        /OBJECT[=<object>]
                        Writes the object module.  This is the default.
                        Without a file name, the object has the source's
                        name and location, with the type OBJ in the same
                        case as the source's type: hello.mar gives
                        hello.obj, HELLO.MAR gives HELLO.OBJ.  A bare file
                        name also goes beside the source (on a volume,
                        in the same device and directory); a host path or
                        a VMS file specification goes where it says.  On
                        a volume, the object is the next version of its
                        name, and the volume must be mounted /WRITE.

        /NOOBJECT       Assembles the source and reports any errors, but
                        writes no object module.

        /LIST[=<listing>]
                        Writes a listing file: the source beside the
                        bytes each line produced, in 60-line pages with
                        headings, then the symbol table, the psect
                        synopsis, the run statistics, and the command
                        line, laid out as VAX MACRO lays out its
                        listings.  The listing is named and placed as the
                        object is, with the type LIS (hello.mar gives
                        hello.lis).  It is written even when the assembly
                        finds errors.  The heading shows the source's
                        full file specification and when it was last
                        revised (a volume file's revision date, or a host
                        file's modification time).

        /NOLIST         Writes no listing.  This is the default.

        /SHOW=(<option>[,...])
        /NOSHOW=(<option>[,...])
                        Turns listing options on or off for the whole
                        listing, whatever the source's .SHOW and .NOSHOW
                        directives say.  The options are BINARY (MEB),
                        the lines of a macro expansion that store bytes;
                        CALLS (MC), macro calls; CONDITIONALS (CND),
                        conditional directives and the lines they leave
                        out; DEFINITIONS (MD), macro definitions; and
                        EXPANSIONS (ME), every line of a macro expansion.
                        By default, all but BINARY and EXPANSIONS are on.

        /CROSS_REFERENCE[=(<option>[,...])]
                        Ends the listing (with /LIST) with a cross
                        reference: for each name, the line that defined
                        it and the lines that referred to it.  The
                        options say what it lists: SYMBOLS, MACROS,
                        OPCODES (instructions), DIRECTIVES, REGISTERS,
                        ALL, or NONE.  Without options it lists symbols
                        and macros.  A reference marked #- is an
                        instruction's register operand, or a symbol in a
                        literal operand (#VALUE).

        /NOCROSS_REFERENCE
                        Writes no cross reference.  This is the default.

        /DEBUG[=(<option>[,...])]
        /NODEBUG
                        Says what the object carries for the debugger and
                        for traceback, the symbolic stack dump VMS prints
                        when a program fails.  The options are TRACEBACK
                        (traceback records), SYMBOLS (debugger records,
                        and no traceback), ALL (both; the same as /DEBUG
                        alone), and NONE.  /NODEBUG is /DEBUG=NONE, which
                        writes no traceback records either.  The default
                        is /DEBUG=TRACEBACK.  Debugger records give the
                        source file, a line-number table of the code, and,
                        with traceback too, a record of each symbol and
                        its data type.  As with VMS MACRO, .ENABLE DEBUG
                        part way through a source starts them there, and
                        .DISABLE DEBUG doesn't stop them.

        /ENABLE=(<function>[,...])
        /DISABLE=(<function>[,...])
                        Turns assembler functions on or off at the start
                        of the source, as .ENABLE and .DISABLE do in it:
                        ABSOLUTE (AMA), DEBUG (DBG), GLOBAL (GBL),
                        SUPPRESSION (SUP), TRACEBACK (TBK), TRUNCATION
                        (FPT), and VECTOR.  The source's own .ENABLE and
                        .DISABLE can still change them.  /DISABLE=TRACEBACK
                        writes no traceback records.  /DEBUG is applied
                        first, then /ENABLE and /DISABLE.

    In the source, .SHOW and .NOSHOW (or .LIST and .NLIST) with options
    turn them on and off; without options they raise and lower the listing
    level, and nothing is listed below level 0.  .PAGE starts a new page,
    and .SBTTL names the pages that follow and adds a line to the table of
    contents, page 0.  .NOCROSS leaves the lines that follow out of the
    cross reference, until a .CROSS; with symbols named, it leaves out just
    those symbols, until a .CROSS names them.

        /LIBRARY=(<library>[,...])
                        Macro libraries (.MLB, the default type) to
                        search for macros the source calls but doesn't
                        define, ahead of STARLET.MLB.  A bare name is found
                        beside the source.  This takes the place of VMS's
                        "MACRO PROG+LIB/LIBRARY".

    Every error in the source is reported, and then no object module is
    written, so a failed assembly never replaces a good object.  Warnings
    are reported, and the object is still written.

    .INCLUDE file names follow the same rules as the source's, and a bare
    name is found beside the source.  So do .LIBRARY names.

    A macro the source calls but doesn't define (a system macro such as
    $EXIT_S or $QIOW_S, say) is looked for in the macro libraries, in VMS
    MACRO's order: those .LIBRARY names, the last named first; then those
    /LIBRARY names, the last named first; then STARLET.MLB.  STARLET.MLB is
    SYS$LIBRARY:STARLET.MLB on a mounted volume, or else STARLET.MLB in the
    host directory the vax.library setting names, or else govax's own.
    govax's own has the RMS macros ($FAB, $RAB, $NAM, the XABs, their
    _STORE forms, and $OPEN, $GET, $PUT, $CLOSE, and the other RMS
    services), the symbol definition macros ($FABDEF, $RMSDEF, $SSDEF,
    $IODEF, and others), and a few system service macros ($EXIT_S,
    $ASSIGN_S, $DASSGN_S, $QIO_S, and $QIOW_S).  Macro libraries are made
    with LIBRARY/MACRO.

    From the host shell, "govax macro <source>" runs this command, with
    --object <file> or --no-object, --list or --list-file <file>,
    --show and --no-show <option,...>, and --library <library> (which may
    be repeated).  --mount DEVICE=container (or
    --mount-write, to allow writing) mounts a container first, and may be
    repeated.  govax exits with a nonzero status if the command fails.

    Examples:

        MACRO "hello.mar"

        MACRO DUA0:[SRC]HELLO /OBJECT=DUA0:[OBJ]

        MACRO "hello.mar"/HOST /OBJECT=DUA0:[000000]HELLO.OBJ

        MACRO "hello.mar"/LIST

        MACRO "prog.mar"/LIBRARY=("mymacros","common.mlb")

        $ govax --mount-write DUA0=work.dsk macro DUA0:[000000]HELLO.MAR

    See also the help for COPY, LIBRARY, MOUNT, and SET DEFAULT.

$LINK

    The LINK command links object modules (.OBJ files, such as MACRO
    writes) into a VAX/VMS executable image (.EXE) that RUN can run.

    Syntax:

        LINK <file>[,<file>...][/HOST] [/EXECUTABLE[=<image>] |
             /NOEXECUTABLE] [/[NO]TRACEBACK] [/[NO]DEBUG] [/[NO]SYSLIB]
             [/MAP[=<map>] [/BRIEF] | /NOMAP]

    Parameter:

        <file>          An object file (.OBJ), or a file with its own
                        qualifier, which follows it:

            <library>/LIBRARY
                        An object library (.OLB) searched for symbols the
                        objects don't define, before the system libraries,
                        or a shareable image library such as IMAGELIB.
            <library>/INCLUDE=(<module>[,...])
                        Adds those modules of the library to the image.
                        With /LIBRARY too, the library is also searched.
            <object>/SELECTIVE_SEARCH
                        Takes only the object's definitions of symbols
                        something else refers to.
            <options>/OPTIONS
                        An options file (.OPT); see below.

                        Files follow MACRO's rules for host files and
                        files on mounted volumes (see HELP MACRO), and a
                        later file's bare name is found beside the one
                        before it.  /HOST applies to every file.

    Qualifiers:

        /EXECUTABLE[=<image>]
                        Writes the image.  This is the default.  Without
                        a file name, the image has the first object's
                        name and location, with the type EXE in the same
                        case as the object's type.  A name that is only a
                        directory gets that name in it.

        /NOEXECUTABLE   Links and reports any errors, but writes no image.

        /TRACEBACK      Starts the image through SYS$IMGSTA, as VMS LINK
                        does by default, and gives it a debug symbol
                        table built from the objects' traceback records.
                        /NOTRACEBACK starts it at its own transfer
                        address, with no debug symbol table.

        /DEBUG          Puts the objects' debugger records (from
                        MACRO/DEBUG) in the debug symbol table too, and
                        marks the image for the debugger, as VMS LINK
                        does.  /DEBUG includes /TRACEBACK, even if
                        /NOTRACEBACK is given.  govax has no debugger,
                        so RUN runs such an image as any other.  A
                        user-written debugger module (/DEBUG=<file>)
                        isn't supported.  /NODEBUG is the default.

        /SYSLIB         Searches IMAGELIB.OLB and STARLET.OLB for symbols
                        the objects don't define, as VMS LINK does by
                        default.  /NOSYSLIB uses only govax's own tables.

        /MAP[=<map>]    Writes a link map, laid out as VMS LINK lays out
                        its default map: the object modules, the program
                        sections and each module's part of them, the
                        global symbols the objects define, and the image
                        synopsis.  Without a file name, the map has the
                        first object's name and location, with the type
                        MAP.  /NOMAP, the default, writes none.

        /BRIEF          Makes the map brief: only the object modules and
                        the image synopsis.

    An options file has a line for each option or list of input files. An
    exclamation point starts a comment, and a hyphen at a line's end
    continues it.  Its files may have /LIBRARY, /INCLUDE, and
    /SELECTIVE_SEARCH, and also /SHAREABLE, a shareable image (.EXE) whose
    symbols the image uses.  A host path is written in quotes, for its
    slashes.  The options are:

        STACK=<pages>           The user stack's size (default 20).
        IDENTIFICATION=<id>     The image's identification.
        NAME=<name>             The image's name in its header.
        SYMBOL=<name>,<value>   Defines a global symbol, whatever the
                                objects say.

    Numbers are decimal, or hexadecimal or octal after %X or %O.

    Program sections are laid out as VMS LINK lays them out: grouped into
    image sections by their attributes, from address 200 (hexadecimal).
    Writable pages nothing is stored in become demand-zero.

    A global symbol the objects don't define is looked up, in order, in:

        IMAGELIB.OLB    Which shareable image defines the routine.  Its
                        offset there comes from the image itself
                        (SYS$SHARE:LIBRTL.EXE, say) or, with no such
                        file, from govax's own tables.
        STARLET.OLB     The module that defines the symbol is added to
                        the image, as VMS LINK does.
        govax's tables  Every LIBRTL routine, and the other run-time
                        library routines govax provides (DECC$..., and
                        others), in their shareable images; STARLET's
                        status codes and other definitions (SS$_...,
                        RMS$_..., IO$_..., and more); and the system
                        services (SYS$...), at their addresses in the P1
                        vector.

    The two libraries and the shareable images are VMS files, which govax
    doesn't include.  LINK looks for them as SYS$LIBRARY:IMAGELIB.OLB,
    SYS$LIBRARY:STARLET.OLB, and SYS$SHARE:<image>.EXE on a mounted
    volume, then in the host directory the vax.library setting names
    (or, if that isn't set, the older vax.link.library).  Without them,
    govax's own tables, which were captured from those files, give the
    same image for everything they define.  Running a routine still
    needs its image, or govax's own routine for it.

    A routine in a shareable image is reached with a general mode operand
    (CALLS #1,G^LIB$PUT_OUTPUT) or a .ADDRESS of it; the image reaches it
    through its fixup section, and RUN connects it to the real image if
    it is there, or else to govax's own routine.

    A symbol nothing defines is a warning, as in VMS LINK: it is 0, each
    reference to it is reported, and the image is still written.  A
    symbol referred to only weakly (.WEAK) is 0 without a warning.  An
    image with no transfer address is written with a warning too.

    With /TRACEBACK, the objects' traceback records become the image's
    debug symbol table, after the image's other blocks, as VMS LINK
    writes it, so a failing program gives VMS's symbolic traceback.
    Debugger records (from MACRO/DEBUG) are skipped unless the link is
    /DEBUG.

    From the host shell, "govax link <object>..." runs this command, with
    --executable <file>, --no-executable, --no-traceback, --debug,
    --no-syslib, --map, --map-file <file>, --brief, --library <file>, or
    --options <file>.

    Examples:

        MACRO "hello.mar"
        LINK "hello"
        RUN "hello.exe"

        LINK DUA0:[OBJ]MAIN,SUB /EXECUTABLE=DUA0:[EXE]PROG.EXE
        LINK "hello"/MAP
        LINK MAIN,MYLIB/LIBRARY,PROG/OPTIONS

    See also the help for MACRO and RUN.

$LIBR

    The LIBRARY command creates, changes, lists, and extracts from VAX/VMS
    libraries, in the style of VMS's LIBRARIAN: macro libraries (.MLB),
    which MACRO searches for macros, and object libraries (.OLB), which
    LINK searches for modules.  govax writes libraries as LIBRARIAN lays
    them out, so a library made here works on VMS, and VMS's work here.

    Syntax:

        LIBRARY <library>[/HOST] [<input>[,...][/HOST]] [qualifiers...]

    Parameters:

        <library>       The library file.  Without a file type it is .OLB,
                        or .MLB with /MACRO.  It can be a host file or a
                        file on a mounted volume, by the rules MACRO uses
                        for its source (see HELP MACRO).

        <input>         Files whose modules go into the library: macro
                        source (.MAR, the default type for a macro
                        library), one module for each macro, or object
                        files (.OBJ, the default for an object library),
                        one module for each object module.  A bare name
                        is found beside the input before it.  With input
                        files and no operation named, their modules
                        replace any of the same names, as LIBRARIAN's
                        default /REPLACE does.

    Qualifiers:

        /CREATE         Creates a new library (a new version on a volume),
                        then inserts any input files' modules.

        /INSERT         Inserts the input files' modules.  A module
                        already in the library is a warning, and is left
                        as it is.

        /REPLACE        Replaces modules with the input files' ones of the
                        same names, inserting the rest.  The default.

        /DELETE=(<module>,...)
                        Deletes modules.  Names may hold the wildcards *
                        and %.  Deletions are done before any insertions.

        /EXTRACT=(<module>,...)
                        Copies modules out of the library, to the file
                        /OUTPUT= names (by default, the library's name
                        with the type .MAR or .OBJ).  Names may hold * and
                        %.

        /LIST[=<file>]  Lists the library: its header, then its modules.
                        Without a file name, the listing goes to the
                        console; a file's default type is .LIS.

        /FULL           With /LIST, adds each module's ident, insertion
                        time, and number of global symbols.

        /NAMES          With /LIST, adds each object module's global
                        symbols.

        /WIDTH=<n>      The width of a /NAMES listing (80 on the console,
                        132 in a file).

        /MACRO          A macro library.  /OBJECT (the default) is an
                        object library.  These choose a new library's
                        type; an existing library keeps its own.

        /NOSQUEEZE      Keeps macros' comments and trailing blanks.  By
                        default they're removed, as LIBRARIAN's /SQUEEZE
                        does, except on .MACRO, .ERROR, .WARN, .PRINT,
                        and .IIF lines.

        /SELECTIVE_SEARCH
                        Marks inserted object modules for selective
                        search: LINK takes from them only symbols already
                        referred to.

        /LOG            Reports each module inserted, replaced, or
                        deleted.

    LIBRARIAN changes a library in place; LIBRARY builds the whole library
    and writes it only when every step has succeeded, so an error leaves
    the library as it was.  A changed library keeps its name and version.

    From the host shell, "govax library <library> [<input>...]" runs this
    command, with --create, --insert, --replace, --delete, --extract,
    --output, --list, --list-file, --full, --names, --macro, --object,
    --no-squeeze, --selective-search, and --log.

    Examples:

        LIBRARY/CREATE/MACRO "mymacros" "mymacros.mar"
        MACRO "prog.mar"/LIBRARY=("mymacros")

        LIBRARY/CREATE DUA0:[LIB]SUBS SUB1,SUB2
        LINK MAIN,DUA0:[LIB]SUBS/LIBRARY

        LIBRARY/LIST/FULL/NAMES DUA0:[LIB]SUBS
        LIBRARY/EXTRACT=(ADD*)/OUTPUT="adds.mar" "mymacros.mlb"

    See also the help for MACRO and LINK.

$SET ,DEFA
$SE  ,DEFA

    The SET DEFAULT command establishes the operator's current default
    device and directory, the same way a partial file specification is
    resolved in real VMS DCL.  DIRECTORY, DELETE, PURGE, COPY, and TYPE
    all fill in any part of a file specification left unspecified (device,
    directory, or both) from this default.

    Syntax:

        SET DEFAULT <spec>

    Parameter:

        <spec>          A device and/or directory specification, such as
                        DUA0:[MYDIR].  Any part omitted (e.g. just
                        [MYDIR], or just DUA0:) is filled in from the
                        *current* default, the same way a real VMS
                        SET DEFAULT works.

    Logical names in <spec> are translated.  The device goes into the
    logical name SYS$DISK, which is how every file command (and a program's
    SYS$OPEN and SYS$CREATE) finds the default device; a concealed logical
    name is kept as the device rather than what it hides.  If <spec> starts
    with a search list, SYS$DISK is set to the search list's name and each
    element is searched in turn.

    Example:

        SET DEFAULT DUA0:[MYDIR]

    See also the help for SHOW DEFAULT.

$SET ,PROM
$SE  ,PROM

    The SET PROMPT command changes the prompt the console shows when it
    reads a command.  The new prompt is also stored as the configuration
    value vax.console.prompt, so it stays in effect in later govax
    sessions.

    Syntax:

        SET PROMPT="<text>"

    Parameter:

        <text>          The new prompt, in double quotes so that its case
                        and any blanks (such as a trailing one) are kept.
                        An empty string ("") returns to the default
                        prompt, "VAX> ".

    The ASM> prompt of the interactive assembler and the debugger's DBG>
    prompt are not changed.

    Examples:

        SET PROMPT="vax$ "

        SET PROMPT=""

$SHOW,SYST
$SH  ,SYST

    The SHOW SYSTEM command lists the processes in the system, as VMS's
    DCL does: each one's process ID, name, scheduling state, current
    priority, and CPU time.  The first line names the system as govax and
    its version, and the node as the host computer's name.  States are VMS's: CUR is the process the CPU
    is running, COM one ready to run, and the wait states are HIB
    (hibernating), LEF and CEF (waiting for a local or common event
    flag), SUSP, and a resource wait such as RWMBX (room in a full
    mailbox).  govax doesn't count I/O operations or page faults, so
    those columns are 0, and Pages counts the pages each process's
    address space maps.  Several processes run only with the
    vax.process.scheduler setting on (HELP CONFIG KEYS).

    Syntax:

        SHOW SYSTEM

    Example:

        GOVAX 1.0-280  on node mymac    7-OCT-2026 11:37:42.07  Uptime  0 00:00:31
          Pid    Process Name    State  Pri      I/O       CPU       Page flts  Pages
        00000301 SYSTEM          CUR      4        0   0 00:00:00.31         0    469

$SHOW,PROC
$SH  ,PROC

    The SHOW PROCESS command displays a process's details: its user,
    process ID and name, terminal, user identification code (UIC), base
    priority, and default device and directory.  With no name or
    /IDENTIFICATION, it shows the console's own process.

    Syntax:

        SHOW PROCESS [process-name] [/IDENTIFICATION=pid]

    Parameter:

        process-name    The name of the process, as SHOW SYSTEM lists
                        it (quote a name with blanks or lowercase).

    Qualifier:

        /IDENTIFICATION=pid
                        The process ID, in hexadecimal, as SHOW SYSTEM
                        lists it.

$SHOW,DEFA
$SH  ,DEFA

    The SHOW DEFAULT command displays the operator's current default
    device and directory, as established by SET DEFAULT.  When the default
    device is a search list, each place it stands for follows on a line
    beginning with "=".

    Syntax:

        SHOW DEFAULT

    See also the help for SET DEFAULT.

$END-OF-FILE
 
